const FULL_GUIDES = {"gypsum":{"title":"Gypsum Products","file":"Topic 3 GYPSUM PRODUCTS.pdf","pages":39,"groups":[[1,6,"Foundations & history"],[7,10,"Production & crystals"],[11,16,"Five gypsum types"],[17,21,"Manipulation & crystallization"],[22,28,"Expansion & setting factors"],[29,33,"Setting time & strength"],[34,36,"Setting time tests"],[37,39,"Cast pouring & infection control"]],"guide":[{"p":1,"title":"Gypsum Products: theory orientation","explanation":"This lecture by Lovely M Annamma covers the mineral, how it is made into dental products, types, physical properties, manipulation, setting and infection control. The important connection is between crystal structure, the amount of water required, and the strength of the finished cast. Study the differences between impression plaster, model plaster, stone and die stone, rather than memorizing five names in isolation.","q":"What is the main structure-property relationship in gypsum?","a":"Particle structure affects water demand, porosity, expansion and strength.","original":"GYPSUM\n  PRODUCTS\n\nLovely M Annamma, BDS, MDS, DipNB, PhD.\n      Department of Prosthodontics\n          Ajman University\n              UAE"},{"p":2,"title":"Roadmap of the lecture","explanation":"The source lists introduction, history, uses, production, classification, properties, manipulation and infection control. Production explains why there are different powders; classification explains what each product is for; setting and manipulation explain why the same powder can produce a better or worse cast. The final part is about protecting laboratory work and personnel from contamination.","q":"What are the major themes of this lecture?","a":"Introduction/history/uses, production/classification, properties/manipulation, infection control.","original":"CONTENT\n\n• INTRODUCTION\n• HISTORY\n• USES\n• PRODUCTION OF\n GYPSUM PRODUCTS\n• CLASSIFICATION\n• PROPERTIES\n• MANIPULATION OF\n GYPSUM PRODUCTS\n• INFECTION CONTROL"},{"p":3,"title":"Gypsum as a mineral","explanation":"Gypsum is calcium sulfate dihydrate, CaSO4·2H2O: a calcium sulfate unit with two waters of crystallization. It occurs naturally as alabaster, selenite, satin spar, rock gypsum and gypsite. Dental gypsum powders are supplied as hemihydrate (half a water per sulfate unit), not the natural dihydrate. They form a dihydrate structure again on setting with water. ADA specification number 25 is the one listed in the lecture.","q":"What is natural gypsum's formula and how is dental powder supplied?","a":"CaSO4·2H2O; supplied as hemihydrate powder.","original":"INTRODUCTION\n\n• Dihydrate form of CaSO4.\n• Natural mineral\n• Basic forms: Alabaster, Selenite,\n  Satin spar, Rock gypsum, Gypsite.\n• Supplied as hemihydrate powders.\n• ADA specification no. 25"},{"p":4,"title":"Historical evidence: gypsum in construction","explanation":"The historical picture panel identifies traces of plaster in Anatolia and Syria approximately 9,000 years ago, Egypt's Cheops pyramid approximately 5,000 years ago, and the association of alabaster gypsum with Solomon's temple. This illustrates the long use of gypsum as a construction and shaping material before dentistry. Inspect the three captions in the original slide.","q":"Which ancient examples are pictured?","a":"Anatolia/Syria plaster, Solomon's temple alabaster, Cheops pyramid.","original":"HISTORY"},{"p":5,"title":"Why it is called plaster of Paris","explanation":"The lecture links the name Plaster of Paris to Montmartre near Paris, where gypsum was extensively manufactured. It describes a French king coating his palace with plaster following the great London fire, and transparent windows made from selenite gypsum. The slide writes 1966 for the London fire; the commonly documented Great Fire was in 1666. The source wording is preserved so the discrepancy is visible, rather than silently treating a slide typo as a verified date.","q":"What is the origin of the name Plaster of Paris, and which transparent form is mentioned?","a":"Manufacture near Montmartre, Paris; selenite.","original":"• The name plaster of Paris – Montematre near to Paris where large\n\n manufacture of gypsum takes place.\n\n• The King of France, after the big London fire destroyed the city in\n\n 1966, covered all his palace with plaster.\n\n• Windows made of transparent gypsum – Selenite Gypsum."},{"p":6,"title":"Five gypsum products by function","explanation":"The five-type chart classifies by use and mechanical/dimensional properties. Type I dental plaster is an impression material; Type II dental plaster is model material; Type III is general-purpose dental stone; Type IV is high-strength, low-expansion die stone; Type V is high-strength, high-expansion die stone. A die represents an individual prepared tooth, so strength, hardness and dimensional fidelity are especially important.","q":"Match Types I-V to their names.","a":"I impression plaster; II model plaster; III dental stone; IV high strength/low expansion; V high strength/high expansion.","original":"GYPSUM PRODUCTS: FIVE-TYPE CLASSIFICATION\n\nClassification according to intended use and mechanical / dimensional properties\n\n       Dental plaster            Dental plaster            Dental stone  1                     2                     3\n       Impression             Model                   General-purpose\n       material                   material                  stone\n\n\n           4                           5\n                                                Dental stone / die\n               Dental stone / die\n                                               High strength • High\n               High strength • Low\n                                                expansion\n               expansion\n\n\n\n    ."},{"p":7,"title":"Calcination: heating and removing water","explanation":"Calcination is controlled dehydration. The source diagram shows dihydrate CaSO4·2H2O heated at 110-130°C to hemihydrate CaSO4·1/2H2O (plaster/stone); at 130-200°C it progresses to hexagonal anhydrite CaSO4; at 200-1000°C to orthorhombic anhydrite CaSO4. Increasing temperature removes water of crystallization and changes crystal phase. The hemihydrate stage is the dental powder stage; anhydrite has no water of crystallization.","q":"What happens to gypsum at 110-130°C in the source diagram?","a":"Dihydrate loses water to form hemihydrate (plaster/stone).","original":"PRODUCTION OF GYPSUM PRODUCTS\n                                                                              HEAT → WATER LOSS →\nProgressive dehydration of gypsum by controlled heating               PHASE CHANGE\n\n         CaSO₄·2H₂O      GYPSUM  C\n  A\n                                            110–130°C   L\n   C      CaSO₄·½H₂O      PLASTER / STONE\n     I\n  N                                        130–200°C\n  A\n         CaSO₄           HEXAGONAL ANHYDRITE   T\n     I\n                                           200–1000°C  O\n  N      CaSO₄          ORTHORHOMBIC ANHYDRITE\n\n\n                Increasing temperature progressively removes water of crystallization and produces\n                                              anhydrite phases."},{"p":8,"title":"Three ways to make dental powders","explanation":"Dry calcination in an open kettle gives beta hemihydrate (Types I and II). Wet calcination in a closed kiln/autoclave gives alpha hemihydrate (Type III). Chemical dehydration: boil in 30% CaCl2 solution and wash with 0.5% sodium succinate to make modified alpha hemihydrate (Types IV and V, improved stone). Synthetic products may come from phosphoric acid manufacture by-products; generally more expensive but properties equal or exceed natural products, according to the slide.","q":"Which manufacturing process makes each hemihydrate form?","a":"Dry/open kettle: beta; wet/autoclave: alpha; boil in chemicals: modified alpha.","original":"Methods of calcination and manufacture of gypsum products\n\n   CONVENTIONAL CALCINATION\n\n   Dry calcination: Open kettle →\n                                          SPECIALIZED METHODS\n1  β-hemihydrate (Types I & II           2\n   gypsum products).                                              Synthetic gypsum products:\n   Wet calcination: Closed                   By-products of phosphoric acid\n    kiln/autoclave → α-                       manufacture; generally more\n   hemihydrate (Type III).                     expensive, with properties\n                                            equal to or exceeding natural   Dehydration by boiling\n                                      gypsum products.    chemicals: 30% CaCl₂ solution,\n    followed by washing with 0.5%\n   sodium succinate → α-\n   modified hemihydrate (Types\n    IV & V), improved stone."},{"p":9,"title":"Look at the three crystal micrographs","explanation":"The left image is beta hemihydrate: irregular, less compact-looking particles. The central alpha image shows denser, more regular prismatic particles. The modified alpha image shows dense, compact particles. The images are not interchangeable; they explain different powder surface and water demand. The interactive crystal model is an illustrative geometry comparison, while these are the actual source micrographs.","q":"Which micrograph corresponds to dry calcination?","a":"Beta hemihydrate, with irregular porous particles.","original":"α- hemihydrate                     Modified α- hemihydrateβ-hemihydrate"},{"p":10,"title":"Alpha versus beta: why water demand matters","explanation":"Alpha crystals are dense and prismatic and need less water to wet particles. Mixing them produces stronger and harder dihydrate. Beta crystals are irregular and spongy, need more water to wet powder particles, and produce weaker/less hard dihydrate. Extra mixing water leaves voids when it is removed, reducing strength. Learn this as the reason dental stone can be stronger than model plaster.","q":"Which form needs less water and produces stronger gypsum?","a":"Alpha hemihydrate.","original":"BETA HEMIHYDRATEALPHA HEMIHYDRATE\n\n                                             • Crystals are characterized by • Crystals are denser and have\n                                           their sponginess and irregular  prismatic shape\n                                  shape\n\n • When alpha hemihydrate is\n                                             • Produce less stronger and  mixed with water, stronger and\n                                     harder dihydrate structure  harder dihydrate forms\n\n • Crystals require less water than\n  beta hemihydrate                      • Crystals require more water to\n                                wet the powder particles"},{"p":11,"title":"Type I: impression plaster","explanation":"Beta calcium sulfate hemihydrate made by dry calcination. Used for mucostatic impression technique and final/wash impressions. Mucostatic means recording tissue in a passive state. Individual type slide: water/powder (W/P) 0.45-0.75, or 45-75 mL per 100 g powder; setting time 4 +/- 1 min; compressive strength 4 MPa. The comparison table on p.16 differs slightly on W/P; retain both.","q":"Recall Type I W/P, setting time and strength.","a":"0.45-0.75; 4 +/- 1 min; 4 MPa (individual slide).","original":"TYPE 1\n\n\n• Impression plaster\n• β-CaSO4 hemihydrate\n                                                              Dry Calcination\n• Mucostatic Impression technique\n• Final impression (wash impression)\n• W/P – 0.45-0.75 (45 mL-75 mL of water per 100g of powder)\n• S.T - 4+/- 1 min\n• Compressive strength - 4 MPa"},{"p":12,"title":"Type II: model plaster","explanation":"Dry-calcined, porous and irregular particles. Also called laboratory plaster/model plaster. It fills a flask in denture construction when setting expansion is not critical and its strength is adequate. Other uses: articulation, study models and diagnostic casts. W/P 0.45-0.50; setting time 12 +/- 4 min; compressive strength 9 MPa. It needs relatively high water due to porous particles.","q":"Name three uses and W/P of Type II plaster.","a":"Articulation, study models, diagnostic casts; W/P 0.45-0.50.","original":"TYPE 2\n                                       Dry Calcination\n• Porous and irregular particles\n• Model plaster, laboratory plaster\n• To fill flask in denture construction when setting expansion is not\n  critical and strength is adequate\n• Uses: Articulation, study models, Diagnostic casts\n• W/P -0.45-0.50\n• S/T - 12±4 min\n• C/S- 9MPa"},{"p":13,"title":"Type III: dental stone","explanation":"Wet calcination produces alpha hemihydrate, more regular and less porous than beta. Higher strength, low expansion, high abrasion resistance per type slide; preferred for casts used to process dentures. Rockwell hardness 82; W/P 0.28-0.30; setting time 12 +/- 4 min; compressive strength 20.7 MPa (table p.16 says 20.6 MPa). Compare its lower water demand with model plaster.","q":"What type is preferred for casts used to process dentures, and its W/P?","a":"Type III; 0.28-0.30.","original":"TYPE 3\n\n• Less porous due to regular crystal form.\n• α- hemihydrate\n• High strength but low expansion\n• High abrasion resistance\n• Preferred for casts used to process dentures.\n• Rockwell hardness -82\n• W/P- 0.28-0.30                               WET CALCINATION\n• S/T- 12±4 min\n• C/S- 20.7MPa"},{"p":14,"title":"Type IV: high-strength, low-expansion die stone","explanation":"Made by boiling with chemicals. Dense, cuboidal particles have lower surface area. A die needs high strength, abrasion resistance, hardness and minimal setting expansion to preserve fit. Other names: class II stone, densite, diestone, improved stone. Rockwell hardness 92; W/P 0.22-0.24; set 12 +/- 4 min; strength 34.5 MPa (p.16 table 34.3 MPa).","q":"Why is Type IV used for dies?","a":"High strength, hardness, abrasion resistance and minimal setting expansion.","original":"CLASS II STONE/ DENSITE/TYPE 4                                        DIESTONE/ IMPROVED STONE\n\n\n                                                                                   BOILING WITH\n• Powder particles are dense and cuboidal in shape               CHEMICALS\n  with reduced surface area.\n• Fabrication of die\n• Die requires high strength ,abrasion resistance, minimum setting\n expansion , hardness\n• Rockwell hardness- 92\n• W/P- 0.22-0.24\n• S/T - 12± 4 min\n• C/S -34.5 MPa"},{"p":15,"title":"Type V: high-strength, high-expansion die stone","explanation":"Similar to Type IV with accelerators, retarders and surface tension reducing agents such as lignin sulfonate. Setting expansion increased from maximum 0.10% to 0.30% to compensate for alloy solidification shrinkage. The slide states not indicated for inlays, where high expansion gives improper fit. W/P 0.18-0.22; set 12 +/- 4 min; strength 48.3 MPa (p.16 table 48.0).","q":"Why is extra expansion useful for Type V, and what is its limitation?","a":"Compensates alloy shrinkage; excessive expansion is unsuitable for inlays in this lecture.","original":"TYPE 5\n\n• Dental stone high strength and high expansion.\n• Same as type 4, but added with accelerators and\n  retarders and surface tension reducing agents like\n  lignin sulfonate.\n• Setting expansion has been increased from a\n maximum of 0.10% to 0.30%\n  to compensate for alloy solidification shrinkage.\n• It is not indicated in inlay, as high expansion leads\n  to improper fit.\n• W/P- 0.18- 0.22\n• S/T - 12± 4 min\n• C/S – 48.3 MPa"},{"p":16,"title":"Read the complete properties comparison","explanation":"The image-only table compares W/P, setting time, porosity, 1-hour compressive strength, abrasion resistance and 2-hour setting expansion. I: 0.40-0.70, 4 +/-1, high, 4.0 MPa, low, 0.15%; II: 0.45-0.50, 12 +/-4, high, 9.0, low, 0.06-0.30%; III: 0.28-0.30, 12 +/-4, moderate, 20.6, moderate, 0.20%; IV: 0.22-0.24, 12 +/-4, low, 34.3, high, 0.15%; V: 0.18-0.22, 12 +/-4, low, 48.0, high, 0.15-0.30%. Table values conflict slightly with individual slides (pp.11-15); use the correct context. Overall trend: water demand and porosity decline as strength increases.","q":"What trend does the p.16 table show as you go from Type II to Type V?","a":"Lower W/P and porosity; higher compressive strength and abrasion resistance.","original":"PROPERTIES OF GYPSUM PRODUCTS"},{"p":17,"title":"Proportioning: follow the water/powder ratio","explanation":"The manufacturer's instructions should always be followed. Strength is inversely related to W/P: more water generally reduces strength because surplus water leaves porosity. The ratio is not a fixed number for every gypsum type. For a powder mass of 100 g, a ratio of 0.30 corresponds to approximately 30 mL of water. This is a mathematical illustration, not a product-specific instruction.","q":"What is the relationship between W/P and strength?","a":"Inverse relationship: increasing W/P generally decreases strength.","original":"MANIPULATION OF GYPSUM PRODUCTS\n\n➢PROPORTIONING:\n• Manufacturer’s instructions should always be followed.\n• Strength of the stone is inversely proportional to the W/P ratio."},{"p":18,"title":"Hand and mechanical mixing","explanation":"Hand mixing uses a rubber bowl and spatula and is usually one minute. The slide mentions an automatic vibrator with high frequency and low amplitude (20-30 sec). Mechanical vacuum mixing produces greater strength and hardness. Vacuum mixing limits trapped air; observe the original equipment pictures. Use recommended mixing conditions for actual products.","q":"What are the hand and mechanical mixing times stated in the lecture?","a":"Hand 1 min; mechanical 20-30 sec.","original":"➢MIXING:\n• Hand mixing: Rubber Bowl and Spatula…..usually 1min.\n• An automatic vibrator of high frequency and low amplitude…..20-30sec.\n• Strength and hardness is more with mechanical vacuum mixing."},{"p":19,"title":"Setting reaction: adding the water back","explanation":"Hemihydrate reacts with water to form calcium sulfate dihydrate, releasing heat; the slide shows a formula with unreacted hemihydrate and heat. The ideal balanced conversion is 2(CaSO4·1/2H2O) + 3H2O -> 2(CaSO4·2H2O). It is exothermic: heat is released, described as similar to heat used during calcination. The lecture says full conversion is not attained unless the material is exposed to high humidity for a long time. Setting is a chemical transformation, not simple drying.","q":"Is setting exothermic or endothermic, and what product forms?","a":"Exothermic; calcium sulfate dihydrate.","original":"Setting Of Gypsum Products\n\n\n• (𝐶𝑎𝑆𝑂4)2. 𝐻2𝑂+ 3𝐻2𝑂→2𝐶𝑎𝑆𝑂4.2𝐻2𝑂+ 𝑢𝑛𝑟𝑒𝑎𝑐𝑡𝑒𝑑𝐶𝑎𝑆𝑂4 2. 𝐻20 +\n 𝐻𝑒𝑎𝑡                              (GYPSUM)\n\n• Exothermic…..similar to heat used during calcination.\n\n• Never attain 100% conversion unless exposed to high humidity for a long time."},{"p":20,"title":"Eight stages of crystallization","explanation":"The physical sequence is dissolution, suspension, saturation, supersaturation, nucleation, growth of nuclei, crystallization and spherulite formation. The lecture groups these into fluid stage (dissolution/suspension), plastic stage (saturation), friable stage (supersaturation/nucleation), carvable stage (crystal growth/crystallization/spherulites). Dissolved material forms new crystals once supersaturated; interlocking crystals produce a rigid mass. Follow the original flowchart and interactive growth model.","q":"What is the sequence from saturation to solid crystal network?","a":"Saturation -> supersaturation -> nucleation -> growth -> crystallization -> spherulites.","original":"STAGES OF CRYSTALLISATION\n\n\n          STAGES OF CRYSTALLISATION                           PHYSICAL STAGES\n\n\n    1    Dissolution                                                                                                                    I. FLUID STAGE\n                                                                   Dissolution and suspension\n    2    Suspension\n\n    3    Saturation                                                                                                                 II. PLASTIC STAGE\n\n                                                                                                                Saturation\n    4    Supersaturation\n\n    5    Nucleation                                                                                                                 III. FRIABLE STAGE\n                                                                                                   Supersaturation and nucleation\n    6   Growth of nuclei\n\n    7     Crystallisation                                                                                      IV. CARVABLE STAGE\n                                                                                                              Crystal growth, crystallisation\n    8    Spherulite formation                                                      and spherulite formation\n\nProgression: dissolution → suspension → saturation → supersaturation → nucleation → crystal growth →\n                                                 crystallisation\n    Dental Materials • Gypsum setting reaction"},{"p":21,"title":"Water/powder ratio controls five properties","explanation":"Low W/P, thick mix: quick setting, high expansion, more strength and surface hardness, less porosity. High W/P, thin mix: slow setting, low expansion, less strength and surface hardness, more porosity. In simple terms, excess water separates particles and leaves pores. The chart shows qualitative trends, not numerical predictions for a specific product.","q":"How does high W/P affect set, expansion, strength, hardness and porosity?","a":"Slower set, lower expansion/strength/hardness, higher porosity.","original":"Effect Of Water/powder ratio On Setting\n\nLow w/p ratio(Thick mix)           High w/p ratio(Thin mix)\n• Quick setting                          • Slow setting\n• High expansion                       • Low expansion\n• More strength                        • Less strength\n• More surface hardness               • Less surface hardness\n• Less porosity                          • More porosity"},{"p":22,"title":"Why gypsum expands when it sets","explanation":"Linear setting expansion can range from 0.06% to 0.5%, depending on product composition. The two kinds are normal and hygroscopic. Dihydrate crystals grow outward from nuclei, intermesh and obstruct adjacent crystals. Mutual outward thrust causes the whole mass to expand even though the reaction itself is rehydration. Learn crystal interlocking rather than assuming expansion comes from water simply swelling the material.","q":"What causes normal setting expansion?","a":"Intermeshing growing dihydrate crystals exert outward thrust.","original":"SETTING EXPANSION\n\n• Depending on composition of gypsum product, linear expansion may\n be as low as 0.06% or high as 0.5%.\n• Normal setting expansion and Hygroscopic setting expansion.\nMechanism:\nThe crystallization of dihydrate can be pictured as an outgrowth of\ncrystals from nuclei of crystallization.\nNuclei   Growing crystals   Intermesh   obstruct growth of adjacent\ncrystals   Outward Thrust   Expansion of entire mass."},{"p":23,"title":"Normal versus hygroscopic expansion","explanation":"Immediately after setting, crystals interlock with micropores and pores that contain excess mixing water. Hygroscopic expansion is additional crystal growth when the crystals have access to water and can grow more freely; the slide says water of hydration is replaced and distance between particles remains the same. This is a setting phenomenon, distinct from merely soaking a fully set cast.","q":"What is hygroscopic setting expansion?","a":"Additional crystal growth/expansion during setting in the presence of water.","original":"Structure immediately after setting is composed of interlocking\ncrystals, between which are micropores and pores containing the\nexcess water required for mixing.\n\n\nHYGROSCOPIC EXPANSION:\n• Additional crystal growth\n• By allowing the crystals to grow freely\n• The water of hydration is replaced and the distance between the\n  particles remains the same."},{"p":24,"title":"Two theories of setting","explanation":"Colloidal theory: hemihydrate particles convert in a sol state to dihydrate crystals; as water is consumed, a colloidal sol becomes a solid gel. Hydration theory: hydrogen bonding links rehydrated plaster particles through sulfate group interactions. The original diagram emphasizes hydration, crystal formation and a rigid interlocking structure. These are the theories as framed by the professor's lecture.","q":"What two theories of the setting reaction are shown?","a":"Colloidal theory and hydration theory.","original":"THEORIES OF SETTING REACTION\n\n\n\n   1 COLLOIDAL                             2\n   THEORY                               HYDRATION\n            Hemihydrate + water                                       THEORYRehydrated plaster particles\nHEMIHYDRATE       SOL           SOLID\n  PARTICLES\n                 STATE           GEL\n\n\n           In the sol state, hemihydrate particles\n\n         convert to dihydrate crystals.\n                                                    Hydrogen bonding\n\n                                                                  links the rehydrated plaster particles\n          As the measured water is consumed,                                                  through interactions with sulfate groups.\n    the mass changes from a colloidal sol to a solid gel.\n\n       KEY IDEA: gypsum setting involves hydration, crystal formation, and development of a rigid interlocking structure.\n\n\n Dental Materials • Setting Reaction"},{"p":25,"title":"Read the expansion-versus-time diagrams","explanation":"This visual-only source compares normal setting (N) with hygroscopic setting (H) at five stages: I initial mix, II initial crystal growth, III solid phase contact, IV expansion, V termination. In the final plot H rises above N, showing greater hygroscopic expansion. The green upper and lower strips illustrate how contact between growing crystals develops; external water permits additional growth. The small graphs are conceptual relative curves, not calibrated expansion percentages.","q":"Which curve has greater final expansion in the slide?","a":"Hygroscopic (H) above normal (N).","original":""},{"p":26,"title":"Temperature has competing effects","explanation":"The lecture says increasing temperature lowers the hemihydrate-to-dihydrate solubility ratio; decreasing temperature reverses that. It also changes ion mobility. The original arrow diagram says higher temperature increases mobility of calcium and sulfate ions, increases reaction rate and decreases setting time. Keep these competing mechanisms separate; the slide does not supply a universal quantitative temperature curve.","q":"What two mechanisms does temperature influence?","a":"Relative solubility of hemihydrate/dihydrate and ion mobility.","original":"Effect Of Temperature On Setting\n\n\nProduces two effects:\n1. As temperature increases, solubility ratio of hemihydrate to\n   dihydrate decreases and vice versa.\n2. Change in ion mobility:"},{"p":27,"title":"Control setting expansion","explanation":"A lower W/P ratio and longer mixing time increase setting expansion. The most effective control is through manufacturer-added chemicals. Potassium sulfate, sodium chloride and borax are named as agents that can reduce setting expansion. Their effects depend on formulation/concentration; this slide is about expansion rather than a blanket statement about setting time.","q":"What manipulation factors increase setting expansion?","a":"Lower W/P and longer mixing.","original":"Control of setting expansion\n\n• A lower W/P ratio and longer mixing time will increase the setting\n expansion.\n• The most effective method is through the addition of chemicals by\n the manufacturer.\n• The setting expansion can be reduced by adding either potassium\n  sulfate, sodium chloride, or borax."},{"p":28,"title":"Manipulation and additives influence strength","explanation":"Mixing improves strength up to a limit (one minute in lecture). Overmixing breaks crystals, leaving a less interlocked structure in the final material. Additives decrease both wet and dry strength according to the slide. The useful idea is that properly developed, interlocked crystals resist loads; more mixing is not always better.","q":"Why does overmixing reduce strength?","a":"It breaks crystals and reduces the final interlocking network.","original":"➢Effect of manipulation:\n• Increase strength to a limit(1min)\n• Overmixing breaks crystals and final product will hold less crystalline\n  interlocking structure.\n➢Effect of additives:\n• Decreases both wet and dry strength."},{"p":29,"title":"Mixing time and working time","explanation":"Mechanical mixing: 20-30 seconds. Hand mixing: one minute. The lecture says three minutes is adequate working time. Mixing time is the time used to prepare the mixture; working time is the usable period before the material becomes too firm. Setting time is a separate measure.","q":"What working time is considered adequate in the source?","a":"3 minutes.","original":"MIXING TIME:       WORKING TIME:\n• Mechanical mixing: 20-30 sec       • 3min is adequate.\n• Hand mixing: 1min"},{"p":30,"title":"Initial versus final setting time","explanation":"Setting time begins at mixing and ends when the material hardens. Initial set is the arbitrary stage when it is a semi-hard mass, no longer workable but not fully set. Final set is when it can be separated from the impression without tear or distortion in the lecture definition. Do not confuse initial loss of workability with readiness to separate a cast.","q":"How do initial and final set differ?","a":"Initial: no longer workable, not fully set; final: can separate without tear/distortion.","original":"SETTING TIME\n\n• From the time of mixing until the material hardens.\n➢Initial setting time: arbitrary stage of firmness which is represented\n by semi-hard mass which is no more workable, but not completely\n  set.\n➢Final setting time: material separated from the impression without\n  tear and distortion."},{"p":31,"title":"Factors affecting setting time","explanation":"The slide lists impurities, fineness, W/P ratio, mixing, temperature, retarders and accelerators, and Terra Alba. Fineness changes surface area; W/P and mixing change contact between particles; temperature affects reaction behavior; added chemicals or impurities can alter rates. Terra Alba is finely divided set gypsum used as nuclei in gypsum products (explanatory context). Learn each factor as distinct rather than treating all changes as accelerators.","q":"Name the seven listed factors.","a":"Impurities, fineness, W/P, mixing, temperature, retarders/accelerators, Terra Alba.","original":"Factors affecting the setting time\n\n• Impurities.\n• Fineness.\n• W/P ratio.\n• Mixing.\n• Temperature.\n• Retarders and accelerators.\n• Terra Alba."},{"p":32,"title":"Accelerators, retarders and balanced stone","explanation":"Accelerators named: gypsum slurry (<20%), potassium sulfate (>2%), sodium chloride (2%). They should increase hemihydrate solubility without increasing dihydrate solubility. Retarders: glue, gelatin and gums adsorb to particles/crystals, decreasing dissolution and crystal growth; borax, potassium citrate and NaCl (20%) form less-soluble calcium salt layers; blood, saliva and alginate also retard. The lecture labels this balanced stone: formulations balance accelerators and retarders. The same salt may behave differently at different concentrations.","q":"Which NaCl concentrations are listed as accelerator and retarder?","a":"2% accelerator; 20% retarder (lecture figures).","original":"Accelerators                          Retarders\n   • Gypsum slurry (<20%)                • Organic materials like glue,\n                                             gelatin, gums.\n   • Potassium sulfate (>2%)\n                                              • They form an adsorbed layer on\n   • Sodium chloride (2%)              hemihydrate to reduce its\n                                              solubility and on gypsum crystal\n   • Should increase the solubility of                                      present to inhibit the growth.\n    hemihydrate without increasing    • Salts like borax, potassium\n                                               citrate, NaCl (20%) form a layer    the solubility of dihydrate\n                                        of calcium salt that is less\n                                        soluble then sulfate .\n                                              • Presence of blood, saliva,\n                                           alginate.BALANCED STONE"},{"p":33,"title":"Wet strength and dry strength","explanation":"Free water in set gypsum distinguishes wet/green strength and dry strength. The slide says dry strength is two times wet strength. Microwave irradiation is described as speeding drying. Understand the comparison as an exam statement rather than a universal exact ratio or permission to microwave all casts. Excess free water makes a set but wet cast more vulnerable.","q":"How does dry strength compare with wet strength in the lecture?","a":"Dry strength is about twice wet/green strength.","original":"Strength of set gypsum products\n\n • Due to the free water content of set gypsum product, two strengths\n  properties are there for gypsum:\n\n\nWet Strength/Green Strength\n Dry Strength(2times greater than wet strength)\n\n • Microwave irradiation speed up drying process."},{"p":34,"title":"Loss-of-gloss test","explanation":"The first initial-set test observes disappearance of the glossy surface as some excess water is taken up during dihydrate formation. The example slide lists 9 min. In the original photographs compare the surface appearance of the gypsum mixes. This is an indicator of initial set, not proof of final strength.","q":"What physical sign indicates initial set in this test?","a":"Loss of surface gloss (lecture example 9 min).","original":"1. Loss of gloss test for initial set\n• Some of the excess water is taken up in forming the dihydrate so that\n the mix loses its gloss.\n• 9min"},{"p":35,"title":"Initial Gillmore needle test","explanation":"The smaller Gillmore needle is used for initial setting time; the time at which it no longer leaves an impression is the initial set. The image lists small needle weight 1/4 lb and diameter 1/12 inch (2.12 mm); large needle weight 1 lb and diameter 1/24 inch (1.06 mm). The heavier/smaller-tip needle serves the later test. Identify the two-needle apparatus from the photo.","q":"Which Gillmore needle is for initial set, with what dimensions?","a":"Small: 1/4 lb, 1/12 inch (2.12 mm).","original":"2. INITIAL GILLMORE TEST\n\n• Smaller one is used.\n• The time at which it no longer leaves an impression is called the\n  initial set."},{"p":36,"title":"Vicat penetrometer test","explanation":"The instrument is the Vicat penetrometer. Setting time is the elapsed time until the needle no longer penetrates to the bottom of the mix. The original picture labels weight 300 g and diameter 1 mm. Compare the measurement criterion with Gillmore, which looks for a surface impression.","q":"What are the Vicat needle weight, diameter and endpoint?","a":"300 g, 1 mm; no longer penetrates to the bottom.","original":"3. VICAT TEST\n\n• Instrument used: Vicat Penetrometer\n• The time elapsed until the needle no longer penetrates to the bottom\n  of the mix is known as the setting time."},{"p":37,"title":"Pouring a gypsum cast","explanation":"The lecture distinguishes anatomical and artistic portions: the anatomical part records the impression, poured using a vibrator; the artistic portion forms the cast base. The listed ways are double pour, single pour and boxing. The vibrator assists flow and bubble control in the impression. The wax lab illustrates beading/boxing as a related method for defining a base.","q":"What are the three listed cast-pouring methods?","a":"Double pour, single pour and boxing.","original":"POURING OF CAST\n•  Anatomical and artistic portion are poured separately.\n•  Anatomical part impression poured using vibrator\n•  Artistic part can be poured in different ways:\n➢Double pour method\n➢Single pour method\n➢Boxing method"},{"p":38,"title":"Infection control of gypsum models","explanation":"The lecture lists iodophor spray or immersion in 1:10 sodium hypochlorite dilution for 10-30 minutes (0.1-1% NaOCl stated). It also describes aseptic wrapping and ethylene oxide sterilization for models from non-disinfected impressions. These are the lecture's figures, not an operational disinfection protocol: actual disinfectants, concentrations, compatibility and contact times depend on validated local procedures and manufacturer instructions. Ethylene oxide is a specialized hazardous sterilization process, not a home or student-lab method.","q":"Which disinfectant categories and immersion time appear in the source?","a":"Iodophor, sodium hypochlorite; 10-30 min in the slide.","original":"INFECTION CONTROL\n\n• Models can be disinfected with a spray iodophor, or by immersion in\n 1:10 dilution of sodium hypochlorite solution for 10 - 30 minutes.\n  (0.1 - 1% concentration of NaOCl)\n• A model poured from a non-disinfected impression can be aseptically\n wrapped and sterilized in ethylene oxide (a common gas used for low\n temperature sterilization)."},{"p":39,"title":"End of lecture: connect the concepts","explanation":"This is the original closing slide. Before moving on, be able to explain the full chain from calcination to particle shape, water demand, crystallization, properties, manipulation and infection control. Use the flashcards below to revisit every source page, including numbers and equipment. Return to the original slides if a value differs between sources.","q":"What connects production to final strength?","a":"Calcination -> crystal morphology -> water demand/porosity -> interlocking and strength.","original":""}]},"theory-impression":{"title":"Dental Impression Materials","file":"Topic 4 Dental impression materials.pdf","pages":49,"groups":[[1,5,"Introduction & classification"],[6,10,"Rigid impression materials"],[11,16,"Zinc oxide eugenol"],[17,24,"Hydrocolloids & alginate"],[25,31,"Elastomers & polysulfide"],[32,35,"Condensation silicone"],[36,39,"Addition silicone"],[40,43,"Polyether"],[44,49,"Techniques, comparison & disinfection"]],"guide":[{"p":1,"title":"Impression Materials: theory orientation","explanation":"This lecture by Lovely M Annamma introduces dental impressions, rigid and elastic materials, chemistry and clinical properties, manipulation, and disinfection. Study the theory and lab lecture separately: the lab handout sometimes gives different labels or timing ranges. The original images show silicones, impression trays and compound cakes.","q":"What are the main families studied in this lecture?","a":"Rigid and elastic impression materials, including hydrocolloids and elastomers.","original":"IMPRESSION MATERIALS\n\n\n\nLovely M Annamma, BDS, MDS, DipNB, PhD.\nDepartment of Prosthodontics\nAjman University\nUAE"},{"p":2,"title":"Impression versus impression material","explanation":"A dental impression is a negative likeness or reversed copy of teeth and adjacent tissues (GPT-9). An impression material is a substance or combination used to create that negative reproduction. A soft material contacts tissues, sets and is removed; cast material then makes a positive replica. The original photos distinguish the negative impression from a positive model.","q":"What is the difference between an impression and a cast?","a":"Impression is a negative copy; cast is a positive replica.","original":"Introduction\n• Dental impression is defined as -A negative likeness or copy in reverse of\n the surface of an object; an imprint of the teeth and adjacent structures\n  for use in dentistry (GPT-9)\n• Impression material is defined as - any substance or combination of\n substances used for making an impression or negative reproduction\n (GPT-9)"},{"p":3,"title":"Historical timeline","explanation":"Waxes, including beeswax, were early oral impression materials in the 18th-19th centuries. Philip Pfaff described softened wax in 1756; Charles Stent introduced impression compound in 1857. Sears used agar for crowns in 1937. Irreversible hydrocolloids appeared early 1940s. S. L. Pearson developed synthetic rubber-base materials in 1955; polysulfide late 1950s, condensation silicone early 1960s, addition silicone 1970s and polyether late 1970s (lecture timeline). Another slide dates polysulfide to 1950; note the source difference.","q":"Who introduced impression compound in 1857, and who used agar in 1937?","a":"Charles Stent; Sears.","original":"History                                    • Sears-1937- First used agar\n                                             hydrocolloids for recording crown• Earliest dental materials were waxes                                             impressions.\n  used in the 18th and 19th centuries.                                                    • Irreversible hydrocolloid are Beeswax is reported as the material                                            introduced in the early 1940s\n  first used in making oral impressions.                                                    • S L Pearson- 1955\n•  Philip Pfaff-1756                                                               - Developed synthetic rubber base\n                                         impression materials      - First described taking impression\n                                                    • Polysulfide - Late 1950’swith softened wax\n                                                    • Condensation Silicone - Early 1960’s• Charles Stent-1857\n                                                    • Addition Silicone - 1970’s\n     - Introduced the first impression                                                    • Polyether - Late 1970’scompound"},{"p":4,"title":"Ideal properties: think patient, handling and accuracy","explanation":"Patient-facing properties: pleasant taste, odor and color, no toxic/irritant ingredients. Practical properties: economical, adequate shelf life, easy handling with minimal equipment. Accuracy properties: dimensional stability; adequate strength to avoid breaking/tearing; elastic recovery with minimal permanent deformation; compatible with die/cast materials and capable of being electroplated. No single material fulfills every property perfectly.","q":"What prevents an impression losing shape on removal?","a":"Adequate strength, elastic recovery and minimal permanent deformation.","original":"Ideal Properties of Impression Materials:\n\n• Have pleasant taste, odor and esthetic color.\n• Not contain any toxic or irritating ingredients.\n• Be economical.\n• Have adequate shelf life for storage and distribution.\n• Be easy to use with minimum equipments.\n• Exhibit dimensional stability.\n• Have adequate strength so that it will not break or tear while\n  removing from the mouth.\n• Posses elastic properties with freedom from permanent\n  deformation after strain.\n• Be able to be electroplated\n• Be compatible with die and cast materials"},{"p":5,"title":"Two-axis classification","explanation":"Chemical setting is irreversible: rigid impression plaster and ZOE; elastic alginate, polysulfide, polyether and silicones. Temperature setting is reversible: rigid compound and waxes; elastic agar hydrocolloid. Classify by both the setting mechanism and elasticity after setting. These two questions distinguish compound from ZOE and agar from alginate.","q":"Which elastic material sets reversibly by temperature?","a":"Agar hydrocolloid.","original":"CLASSIFICATION\n\n\n Based on Setting and Elasticity:\n\nMODE OF SETTING        RIGID                   ELASTIC\n\n\n\n Set by chemical reaction   Impression plaster, Zinc     Alginate,\n (irreversible)              oxide eugenol               Polysulfide,Polyether,Silico\n                                             ne\n\n Set by temperature       Compound, Waxes        Agar hydrocolloid\n change (reversible)"},{"p":6,"title":"Impression plaster","explanation":"Type I gypsum product, rigid after setting. Used for mucostatic final impressions for edentulous patients, especially displaceable soft tissues recorded in a passive state. The source photos show the clinical stages. A mucostatic material aims to record soft tissue without displacement.","q":"Why use a mucostatic impression material?","a":"To record soft tissues in their passive/non-displaced state.","original":"IMPRESSION PLASTER\n\n• Basically a GYPSUM product (Type-1)\n• Used as Mucostatic impression material for making final impressions\n  for edentulous patients\n• Applicable to patients with displaceable soft tissues that should be\n recorded in a passive state"},{"p":7,"title":"Impression plaster: composition and properties","explanation":"Beta calcium sulfate hemihydrate; soluble plaster/potato starch; potassium sulfate reduces expansion and accelerates setting; borax retards; diatomaceous earth, quartz and lime make it more brittle. Advantages: cheap, long shelf life, easy preparation, very good detail and excellent dimensional stability. Disadvantages: rigidity, fracture with undercuts, possible soft-tissue drying and discomfort, difficult cast separation.","q":"Which additives accelerate and retard setting?","a":"Potassium sulfate accelerates; borax retards.","original":"Advantages\n                                        1.  Cheap and long shelf life, easy toComposition\n                                     prepare\n                                        2.  Very good surface detail\n• CaSO4 .½H2O - β Hemihydrate       reproduction\n• Soluble plaster (potato starch)                                        3.  Excellent dimensional stability\n• Potassium sulfate - reduce\n expansion & accelerates setting                                  Disadvantages\n• Borax - a retarder to prolong                                    1.Very rigid -  setting time\n                                        2. Fractures if undercuts are present• Diatomaceous earth, quartz,\n                                        3. May dry soft tissues, unpleasant to  lime - to make the plaster more\n  brittle                                    patients\n                                   4.Separation from cast may become\n                                            difficult"},{"p":8,"title":"Impression compound: thermoplastic","explanation":"Also called modelling plastic; rigid and reversible, sets by physical change. ADA specification 3. Supplied as red cakes or green/gray/red sticks. Applications: preliminary edentulous impression, making a special tray, border molding. The physical state changes with heating and cooling; this does not make it elastic when set.","q":"What three applications are listed for impression compound?","a":"Preliminary edentulous impressions, special trays, border molding.","original":"Impression Compound\n\n• Also called “modelling plastic”\n• It is a rigid, reversible impression material which sets by physical\n change\n• Thermoplastic material\n• ADA specification no. 3\n• Supplied in the form of cakes(red) and sticks (green, gray or red)\nApplications :1) Making preliminary impression of edentulous mouth\n              2) To make a special tray\n              3) Border Moulding"},{"p":9,"title":"Fusing-temperature classification of compound","explanation":"Theory classification: Type I low fusing 50-60°C for detailed impressions and border molding; Type II high fusing above 70°C for special tray compound; medium fusing 60-70°C for edentulous arch impressions. The separate lab labels brown cake Type I and green stick Type II. These are different conventions in the supplied sources; do not equate green stick with high fusing simply from the lab label.","q":"What are the theory fusing-temperature categories?","a":"Low 50-60°C (Type I), medium 60-70°C, high >70°C (Type II).","original":"• 2 types of Impression compound according to fusing\n temperature:\n   Type I : Low fusing {50-60 ℃}\n      -For detailed impressions and border molding\n   Type II : High fusing {above 70℃}\n       - Tray compound for special impression trays\n• Medium fusing {60-70 ℃} - impression of edentulous arches"},{"p":10,"title":"Impression wax: corrective and bite registration","explanation":"Primarily edentulous regions; high flow at mouth temperature, hence mouth-temperature waxes. Ingredients: beeswax, paraffin and ceresin. Corrective wax: 100% flow at 37°C, functional impressions for partial dentures/obturators, record posterior palatal seal (PPS), applied as veneer over original impression for soft-tissue detail. Bite registration wax: 2.5-22% flow at 37°C; U-shaped thin sheets, sometimes metallized or foil laminated; record occlusal relationship.","q":"Compare the two flow percentages at 37°C.","a":"Corrective 100%; bite registration 2.5-22%.","original":"Impression Wax\n• Primarily used to make impressions of edentulous regions of the mouth\n• They exhibit high flow at mouth temperatures, hence the name ‘mouth temperature waxes’.\n• Composition : Bees wax, Paraffin wax and Ceresin wax\n• Types:\n\n CORRECTIVE                                         BITE REGISTRATION\n\n\n\n\n\n  •  Flow is 100% at 37 ℃\n  •  Functional impression in partial dentures and\n    obturators.                                     • Flow is 2.5% to 22% at 37°C\n  •  Record PPS                                     • U shaped thin sheets, sometimes metallized or\n  •  Used as wax veneer over original impression to                                                             foil laminated.\n    register details of soft tissues.                                                    • Record occlusal relationship."},{"p":11,"title":"ZOE impression paste: uses","explanation":"Zinc oxide eugenol paste is rigid after setting. Applications: secondary edentulous impression, temporary denture relining, bite registration and surgical pack paste for periodontal wound healing. The photographed product is the two-tube impression paste; the related cement lab shows other ZOE formulations.","q":"List four uses of ZOE impression paste.","a":"Secondary impression, temporary relining, bite registration, periodontal surgical pack.","original":"Zinc-oxide Eugenol Impression Paste\n\nAPPLICATIONS:\n• Secondary Impression material for\n edentulous patients.\n• Temporary relining material for dentures.\n• Bite registration paste.\n• Surgical pack paste for periodontal wound\n  healing."},{"p":12,"title":"ZOE composition table","explanation":"Tube 1 base: zinc oxide 87%, fixed vegetable/mineral oil 13%. Tube 2 accelerator: oil of cloves/eugenol 12%, gum/polymerized rosin 50%, silica-type filler 20%, lanolin 3%, resinous balsam 10%, accelerator solution (CaCl2) 5%. The two pastes supply the reactive ingredients plus oils/resins/fillers that control handling. Learn the separate tube percentages, not a single combined mixture.","q":"What are the main ingredients and percentages in base and accelerator?","a":"Base ZnO 87%, oil 13%; accelerator eugenol 12%, rosin 50%, filler 20%, lanolin 3%, balsam 10%, CaCl2 solution 5%.","original":"Composition\n  • Supplied in two separate pastes dispensed in tubes:\n\n    COMPONENTS                                   PERCENTAGE\n\n     Tube no 1 (Base)\n      Zinc oxide                                         87\n      Fixed vegetable or mineral oil                          13\n\n     Tube no 2 (Accelerator)\n       Oil of cloves or eugenol                               12\n    Gum or polymerised rosin                             50\n        Filler (silica type)                                    20\n\n      Lanolin                                           3\n      Resinous balsam                                    10\n      Accelerator solution (CaCl2)                           5"},{"p":13,"title":"ZOE setting reaction","explanation":"The reaction is ionic and requires a medium for ions. First ZnO + H2O -> Zn(OH)2 (hydrolysis). The weak acid phenolic OH of eugenol reacts with zinc hydroxide: Zn(OH)2 + 2HE -> ZnE2 + 2H2O, where HE represents eugenol and ZnE2 zinc eugenolate salt. Water starts the reaction and is also produced, so the slide describes it as autocatalytic. This is an acid-base setting reaction, not thermal softening.","q":"What salt is formed and why is the reaction called autocatalytic?","a":"Zinc eugenolate; water initiates the reaction and is regenerated.","original":"Setting Reaction\n• Ionic in nature\n• Requires ionic medium in which it can proceed at any desired rate\n• 1st Reaction -Hydrolysis of zinc oxide to its hydroxide form\n• When 2 pastes are mixed, the phenol -OH of the eugenol acts as a weak acid and\n  undergoes an acid-base reaction with zinc hydroxide\n• Forms a salt- zinc eugenolate.\n• As water is used for initiation and obtained as a by-product, the reaction can also be\n  described as an autocatalytic reaction."},{"p":14,"title":"ZOE setting times and measurable properties","explanation":"Type I and II initial set 3-6 min; final set Type I 10 min, Type II 15 min. ADA specification 16 spread: Type I 30-50 mm, Type II 20-45 mm. Compressive strength about 7 MPa at 2 h; good flow gives accurate detail; negligible shrinkage under 0.1% during hardening. Distinguish flow before set from rigidity after set.","q":"Compare Type I and II final setting time and spread.","a":"Type I 10 min, 30-50 mm; Type II 15 min, 20-45 mm.","original":"Setting Time :\n\n                                                 Initial setting time         Final setting time\n\n           Type I                    3-6 min                10 min\n\n           Type II                   3-6 min                15 min\nProperties:\n• CONSISTENCY AND FLOW:\n\n  According to ADA specification No. 16, the spread is:\n\n   - Type I - 30 to 50 mm\n\n    - Type II - 20 to 45 mm\n• RIGIDITY AND STRENGTH:\n\n  The compressive strength of hardened ZOE is approximately 7MPa two hours after mixing.\n• DETAIL REPRODUCTION:\n\n     It registers surface details quite accurately due to the good flow\n• DIMENSIONAL STABILITY:\n\n  A negligible shrinkage (less than 0.1%) may occur during hardening."},{"p":15,"title":"ZOE advantages, disadvantages and tolerance","explanation":"Some patients experience burning because of eugenol. Advantages: low viscosity (does not compress soft tissue in lecture framing), dimensional stability (<0.1% shrinkage), good detail and low price. Disadvantages: not usable in deep undercuts and eugenol allergy in some patients. Non-eugenol alternatives are shown in the lab lecture.","q":"What are the two major disadvantages listed?","a":"Deep undercuts are unsuitable; possible eugenol allergy/irritation.","original":"• BIOLOGICAL CONSIDERATIONS:\n\nSome patients experience a burning sensation in the mouth due to eugenol.\n\n\nAdvantages:\n• Low viscosity - no compression of soft tissues\n• Dimensional stability ( shrinkage less than 0.1%)\n• Good surface detail reproduction\n• Low price\nDisadvantages:\n• Cannot be used in deep undercuts\n• Eugenol allergy in some patients"},{"p":16,"title":"ZOE manipulation sequence","explanation":"Mix on oil-impervious paper or glass slab. Express two strips of equal length, one from each tube. Use flexible stainless-steel spatula; combine contrasting colors with the first stroke and mix approximately 1 min until uniform color. The original photo panel shows separate pastes, mixed color and tray. Equal lengths and uniform color are the lecture's preparation cues; actual product proportion depends on its instructions.","q":"How do you recognize a completed ZOE mix in the lecture?","a":"Uniform color after about 1 min of mixing equal strips.","original":"Manipulation\n\n• Mixed on oil impervious paper or glass mixing slab\n• Proper proportion of two pastes obtained by\n squeezing 2 strips of paste of the same length,one\n from each tube ,onto the mixing slab\n• Flexible stainless steel spatula used for mixing 2\n  strips of contrasting colors combined with the first\n  stroke of the spatula ,mixing is continued for\n approx 1 min, until a uniform color achieved."},{"p":17,"title":"Elastic material family tree","explanation":"The diagram divides elastic impression materials into aqueous hydrocolloids (agar, alginate) and non-aqueous elastomers (polysulfides, silicones, polyethers). Elastic materials can deform during removal and recover; not all have equal stiffness or recovery. Aqueous means water-based, while elastomers form rubber-like networks.","q":"What are the aqueous hydrocolloids?","a":"Agar and alginate.","original":"Elastic Impression Materials\n\n\n\n                                               Elastic\n                                     Impression\n                                       Materials\n\n\n                                                    Non-aqueous\n             Aqueous                                     elastomeric\n              Hydrocolloids                                  impression\n                                                                materials\n\n\n\n      Agar-agar           Alginate          Polysulfides          Silicones          Polyethers"},{"p":18,"title":"Agar conditioning equipment","explanation":"Agar impression tray material requires a three-compartment conditioning unit for liquefaction, storage and tempering. Syringe material uses only liquefaction and storage compartments. Liquefaction makes the gel flow; storage maintains the sol; tempering prepares the tray material for use. The pictures identify the conditioning unit, hoses and tray. Study the difference in paths through the unit.","q":"Name the three compartments and the syringe exception.","a":"Liquefaction, storage, tempering; syringe material uses the first two.","original":"Making the Agar Impression\n\n• Process requires a 3 compartment conditioning unit for the agar tray\n  material\n• Allows liquefaction,storage and tempering\n• Syringe material used only in liquefaction and storage compartments"},{"p":19,"title":"Alginate introduction","explanation":"Irreversible elastic hydrocolloid; mucous extract from certain brown seaweeds; widely used and developed as an agar substitute. Type 1 fast setting and Type 2 normal setting. Powder mixed with water undergoes chemical gelation. Once set it cannot be converted back just by warming.","q":"Why is alginate called irreversible?","a":"Its chemical gelation cannot be undone by a temperature change.","original":"Alginate\n\n• Irreversible elastic hydrocolloid\n• Mucous extract yielded from certain brown seaweeds.\n• Most widely used impression material\n• Developed as a substitute for agar\n• Types:\n    Type 1- Fast Setting\n    Type 2 - Normal Setting"},{"p":20,"title":"Alginate applications","explanation":"Used for impression making when there are undercuts or excessive saliva, for study models and working casts, and preliminary impressions. The photo shows an impression with tooth-shaped recesses. Elasticity helps removal around undercuts; its dimensional limitations explain the need for timely cast pouring.","q":"What are common applications of alginate?","a":"Preliminary impressions, study models/working casts, cases with undercuts or excess saliva.","original":"Applications :\n• Used for impression making;\n     - when there are undercuts\n     - in mouth with excessive flow\nof saliva\n• For impression to make study\n models and working casts\n• For making preliminary\n impression"},{"p":21,"title":"Alginate composition and functions","explanation":"Sodium/potassium alginate 15% (reacts with calcium ions); calcium sulfate 16% reactor; zinc oxide 4% filler; potassium titanium fluoride 3% gypsum hardener; diatomaceous earth 60% filler; sodium phosphate 2% retarder reacting with calcium sulfate; coloring/flavoring agents such as wintergreen and orange. Filler is the largest listed fraction. The retarder gives handling time before the insoluble gel forms.","q":"What is the largest ingredient fraction and what delays setting?","a":"Diatomaceous earth 60%; sodium phosphate 2% retarder.","original":"Composition\n\nINGREDIENTS                  PERCENTAGE                  FUNCTION\n\n\nSodium or potassium alginate      15%                              React with calcium ion\n\n\nCalcium sulphate (reactor)        16%                              React with potassium alginate\n\n\nZinc oxide                  4%                                 Acts as filler\n\n\nPotassium titanium flouride       3%                          Gypsum hardener\n\n\nDiatomaceous earth            60%                                Acts as filler\n\n\nSodium phosphate(retarder)      2%                               React with calcium sulphate\n\n\nColouring and flavouring agents                                        Wintergreen, orange etc."},{"p":22,"title":"Alginate's two reactions and mixing","explanation":"First 2Na3PO4 + 3CaSO4 -> Ca3(PO4)2 + 3Na2SO4; the retarder consumes calcium first and delays gelation. Then sodium alginate + CaSO4 + H2O -> calcium alginate + Na2SO4 (schematic polymer reaction). Add measured powder to premeasured water; mix with vigorous figure-eight motion, stropping against rubber bowl sides; theory mix 45 sec-1 min. The source photos show consistency changing through mixing and set.","q":"Why does the phosphate reaction happen first?","a":"It uses available calcium to delay gelation and create working time.","original":"• Manupulation\n                                          • The measured powder is sifted\nSetting Reaction                                      into pre-measured water\n                                          • A vigorous figure - 8 motion,\n• Two main reaction occur                                  with the mix being stropped\n•        +                         against the sides of rubber 2Na3PO4  3CaSO4\n                                  mixing bowl Ca3(PO4)2 + 3Na2SO4\n                                          • Mixing time between 45 secs - 1• Here firstly the retarder is\n                             min completely get reacted to delay\n the reaction time.\n• Sodium alginate +      +                CaSO4  H2O\n Calcium Alginate + Na2SO4"},{"p":23,"title":"Alginate properties and dimensional changes","explanation":"Syneresis and imbibition change dimensions; cooling from mouth 37°C to room 23°C causes slight shrinkage. Good elasticity and elastic recovery, high flexibility/flow, fine tissue detail per theory. The source gives compressive strength 500-8000 gm/cm² and tear strength 350-700 gm/cm² (source units/ranges). Lab calls detail poor relative to precision materials; keep the comparison context.","q":"What two water processes and two temperatures are listed?","a":"Syneresis, imbibition; mouth 37°C, room 23°C.","original":"PROPERTIES:\n\n1)Dimensional Stability:\n•  Dimensional change due to syneresis and imbibition.\n•  Impression shrink slightly due to thermal differential between mouth\n temperature(37℃)and room temperature(23℃)\n2)Good elasticity and elastic recovery\n3)Flow: Highly flexible\n4)Accuracy: Fine reproduction of tissue detail\n5)Compressive strength= 500-8000 gm/cm2\n  Tear strength = 350-700 gm/cm2"},{"p":24,"title":"Alginate compatibility, timing and trade-offs","explanation":"Gelation produces sodium sulfate; low concentrations accelerate gypsum setting, high concentrations retard. Potassium titanium fluoride surface hardener counteracts this. Normal set working 2 min, setting 4.5 min; fast working 1.25 min, setting 1-2 min (theory). Advantages: easy manipulation, no expensive equipment, low cost and comfort. Disadvantages: high viscosity, tissue displacement, easy tearing and poor dimensional stability.","q":"Give theory normal and fast alginate working/setting times.","a":"Normal 2/4.5 min; fast 1.25/1-2 min.","original":"• Advantages\n   Properties                              • Ease of manipulation\n                                                            • No need of expensive equipments     6)Compatibility with gypsum:\n                                                            • Relatively low cost      •  Gelation produces sodium sulfate\n      which acts as an gypsum-setting               • Comfort to patients\n       accelerator at low concentrations and a\n       retarder at higher concentrations.\n      • Counteracted by gypsum surface                                     Disadvantages:\n      hardener - Potassium titanium fluoride\n                                                            • High viscosity\n                                                            • Ability to displace tissues\n                 Normal Set          Fast set alginate\n                                                            • Tears easily                     Alginate\n                                                            • Poor dimensional stabilityWORKING TIME     2 min          1¼ min\nSETTING TIME     4½ min             1-2 min"},{"p":25,"title":"Elastomer classification","explanation":"The original tree lists silicone (condensation and addition), polysulfide and polyether. All are non-aqueous elastic impression materials. Condensation polymerization releases a by-product; addition systems generally do not release a reaction by-product during the main reaction. Learn the family tree before the individual reaction mechanisms.","q":"Name the four elastomer groups after splitting silicones.","a":"Polysulfide, polyether, condensation silicone and addition silicone.","original":"ELASTOMERS\n\n\n\n\n\n             SILICONE         POLYSULPHIDE        POLYETHER\n\n\n\n\n\nCONDENSATION        ADDITION"},{"p":26,"title":"Elastomer consistencies","explanation":"Increasing filler content gives extra light, light, medium/regular, heavy and putty (extra heavy) bodies. Low viscosity can flow into fine tooth detail; high viscosity provides bulk and tray support. The photographed kit illustrates putty, light body and catalyst packaging. Body names concern consistency, not a separate material chemistry.","q":"Put the body grades in order of increasing filler content.","a":"Extra light, light, medium, heavy, putty.","original":"Elastomers\n\n• Formulated in several consistencies in increasing content of filler:\n   Extra light body\n   Light body\n  Medium or regular body\n  Heavy body\n   Putty (extra heavy)"},{"p":27,"title":"Polysulfide introduction and applications","explanation":"The slide calls polysulfide the first synthetic elastomer introduced in 1950 (history slide says late 1950s). Two-paste system in low, medium and high consistency. Brand examples COE-FLEX, Permlastic (lead dioxide system), Omniflex (copper hydroxide system). Used for crowns, bridges, inlays, onlays and partial dentures. The photo helps recognize a two-paste kit.","q":"What is polysulfide supplied as and what are its uses?","a":"Two pastes; crown, bridge, inlay/onlay and partial denture impressions.","original":"Polysulphides\n• First synthetic elastomeric impression material introduced in 1950\n• 2 paste system\n• Available in low, medium and high consistencies\n• Brands - COE- FLEX, Permlastic (Lead dioxide system), Omniflex\n (Copper hydroxide system)\n• Used to record impressions for crowns,bridges , inlays , and onlays\n and partial dentures."},{"p":28,"title":"Polysulfide composition: read the original values","explanation":"Base: mercaptan (-SH) polysulfide polymer 80-85% principal ingredient; the table lists titanium oxide/zinc-related fillers 16-18%, strengthener (sulfate, copper carbonate or silica row) 2%, dibutyl phthalate plasticizer 30-35%. Accelerator: lead dioxide 60-68% reactor, sulfur 0.5% promoter, oleic/stearic acid retarder. The base numbers as printed exceed 100% and the row alignment is ambiguous: treat them as lecture entries, not a valid recipe. The original table remains visible.","q":"Which group reacts and which accelerator reactor is named?","a":"Mercaptan -SH groups; lead dioxide reactor.","original":"Composition\n\n\nBase Paste                      Function                            Percentage\n\nPolysulfide polymer(-               Principal ingredient                 80-85%\nSH,mercaptan group)\n\nTitanium oxide and Zinc              Fillers                            16-18%\n\nSulphate, Copper carbonate or     Strengthener                  2%\nsilica\n\nDibutyl phthalate                    Plasticizer (confers viscosity to base)    30-35%\n\n\nACCELERATOR PASTE\n\nLead dioxide                     Reactor                          60-68%\n\nSulfur                           Promoter, accelerates the reaction     0.5%\n\nOleic acid or stearic acid           Retarder, controls setting reaction"},{"p":29,"title":"Polysulfide setting reaction","explanation":"Mix base and accelerator; reaction starts at mixing and reaches maximum rate soon after spatulation. Cross-linking forms a resilient network. The source schematic: mercaptan + lead oxide -> polysulfide + water. Water is the by-product, which is relevant to dimensional stability. The network gives elastic behavior.","q":"What is the reaction by-product of polysulfide?","a":"Water.","original":"Setting reaction\n\n• Base and acceleration paste are mixed .\n• The reaction starts at the beginning of mixing and reaches its\n maximum rate soon after spatulation is complete.\n• A Resilient network has started to form.\n• The reaction yields water as a by-product\n  Mercaptan + lead oxide            Polysulfide + water"},{"p":30,"title":"Polysulfide properties","explanation":"Theory working time 3-6 min, setting 7-10 min (longest in source). Excellent surface detail, very good tear resistance, good flexibility, highest permanent deformation, hydrophobic, biocompatible, good shelf life. Poor dimensional stability due to by-product loss. The comparison table p.46 lists a different working range (4-7 min), and the lab has its own values.","q":"Why is polysulfide less dimensionally stable?","a":"Loss of water by-product and relatively high permanent deformation.","original":"Properties\n• Working time=3-6 min\n• Setting time= 7-10 min (longest)\n• Excellent reproduction of surface detail\n• Very good tear resistance\n• Not Dimensionally stable- due to loss of by-product\n• Good flexibility\n• Shows highest permanent deformation\n• Biocompatible\n• Hydrophobic\n• Good shelf life"},{"p":31,"title":"Polysulfide advantages and disadvantages","explanation":"Advantages: high tear strength, long working time, established precision, economical, extensive shelf life, less hydrophobic (relative). Disadvantages: dimensional instability, unacceptable odor, untidy/stains clothing, long setting time, least elastic recovery and subsequent pours less accurate. Its strength in resisting tears does not imply best elastic recovery.","q":"Which material has high tear resistance but poor elastic recovery?","a":"Polysulfide.","original":"DisadvantagesAdvantages\n\n                                             •  Dimensionally unstable• High tear strength\n                                             •  Unacceptable odor• Long working time\n                                             •  Untidy and stains clothing\n• Established precision                                             •  Long setting time\n• Economic                                             •  Least elastic recovery\n• Extensive shelf life                      •  Subsequent pours are less\n• Less hydrophobic                      accurate"},{"p":32,"title":"Condensation silicone: presentation and uses","explanation":"Conventional silicone or room-temperature-vulcanizing (RTV) silicone. Two pastes, base paste plus low-viscosity liquid catalyst, or two putties. Putty combined with low-viscosity silicone is the putty-wash technique. Brand Speedex. Uses include crowns, bridges, inlays, onlays and complete dentures. The image shows a putty tub, base and catalyst.","q":"What is RTV silicone and what is the putty-wash technique?","a":"Condensation silicone; supporting putty combined with low-viscosity silicone wash.","original":"Condensation Silicone\n\n• Also known as conventional silicones or room\n temperature vulcanizing (RTV) silicones.\n• Supplied as two-paste system or base-paste and a low\n  viscosity liquid catalyst or a two- putty system\n• Putty used as tray material in conjunction with a low-\n  viscosity silicone\n• Referred to as the Putty-wash technique\n• Brands- Speedex\n• Used to record impressions for crowns,bridges, inlays,\n onlays and complete dentures."},{"p":33,"title":"Condensation silicone ingredients and reaction","explanation":"Base: hydroxyl-terminated polydimethylsiloxane low-molecular-weight polymer plus colloidal silica or titanium dioxide fillers. Liquid accelerator: alkyl silicate cross-linker, stannous octoate activator. Condensation of hydroxy-terminated PDMS with tetraethyl orthosilicate in the presence of stannous octoate creates cross-links and releases ethanol. The alcohol by-product can evaporate.","q":"Which catalyst and by-product are listed?","a":"Stannous octoate; ethanol (ethyl alcohol).","original":"Composition\n\nBASE PASTE                                 FUNCTION\n\nHydroxyl- terminated polydimethyl siloxane           low molecular weight silicone polymer\n\nColloidal silica or titanium dioxide                            Fillers\n\nLIQUID ACCELERATOR\n\nAlkyl silicate                                              Cross-linking\n\nStannous Octoate                                       Activator\n\n\nSetting Reaction\n•  Curing involves a reaction of tri- and tetra-functional alkyl silicates in the presence of stannous octoate as a\n    catalyst\n•  Condensation polymerisation of hydroxy -terminated polydimethyl siloxane occurs with tetraethyl\n    orthosilicate in the presence of stannous octoate(catalyst)\n•  This reaction results in the release of ethanol molecules\n•  By-product: Ethyl alcohol"},{"p":34,"title":"Condensation silicone properties","explanation":"Working time 2.5-4 min, set 6-8 min. Pleasant odor/color in theory (lab describes strong odor), highly elastic, excellent detail, hydrophobic requiring dry field, biologically inert, compatible with gypsum. Lesser dimensional stability: curing shrinkage 0.4-0.6%; permanent deformation reported 1-3% with evaporation of ethyl alcohol. Volatile by-product loss explains prompt pouring.","q":"What are theory setting time and curing shrinkage?","a":"6-8 min; 0.4-0.6%.","original":"Properties\n\n• Working time=2.5 - 4 min\n• Setting time= 6-8 min\n• Pleasant odor and color.\n• Excellent reproduction of surface details and highly elastic.\n• Lesser dimensional stability\n          - high curing shrinkage (0.4 - 0.6%)\n          - permanent deformation due to shrinkage caused by the evaporation of ethyl\nalcohol is also high (1-3%).\n• Hydrophobic - needs a dry field ..\n• Biologically inert.\n• Compatible with all gypsum products."},{"p":35,"title":"Condensation silicone trade-offs","explanation":"Putty-wash advantages: putty for custom tray, clean/pleasant, good working time, easily visible margins. Disadvantages: high polymerization shrinkage, volatile by-product, low tear strength, hydrophobic and need to pour immediately. Lab specifies first 30 min. Learn the reason for the short pouring interval: alcohol escapes and dimensions can change.","q":"Why pour condensation silicone promptly?","a":"Volatile alcohol by-product and polymerization shrinkage reduce dimensional stability.","original":"Advantages(putty wash)\n\n• Putty for custom tray\n• Clean and pleasant\n• Good working time\n• Margins easily seen\nDisadvantages:\n• High polymerisation shrinkage\n• Volatile by-product\n• Low tear strength\n• Hydrophobic\n• Pour immediately"},{"p":36,"title":"Addition silicone: PVS","explanation":"Also polyvinyl siloxane or vinyl polysiloxane (PVS). Two-paste system, single phase/monophase, low/medium/high/very high consistencies. Brand Aquasil; photo Flexceed kit. Used for crowns, inlays, onlays, partial and complete dentures. Unlike condensation silicone, the main addition reaction has no volatile condensation by-product.","q":"What does PVS stand for and how is it supplied?","a":"Polyvinyl siloxane; two-paste system.","original":"Addition Silicones\n\n• Commonly referred to as Polyvinyl siloxanes(PVS) or Vinyl poly\n  siloxane\n• 2 PASTE SYSTEM\n• Single phase / Monophase\n• Supplied as low, medium, high and very high consistencies\n• Brand name- Aquasil\n• Used to record impression for crowns,\n  inlays, onlays, partial and complete dentures."},{"p":37,"title":"Addition silicone composition and reaction","explanation":"Base: polymethylhydrosiloxane low-molecular-weight polymer and fillers. Accelerator: divinylpolysiloxane, platinum salt catalyst and retarder controlling work/set. Vinyl siloxane + silane siloxane -> silicone rubber in presence of platinum salt (chloroplatinic acid in slide). Main reaction releases no by-products. Residual hydrides can react with moisture/impurities, releasing hydrogen gas as a secondary reaction.","q":"What is the catalyst and which gas can appear from a secondary reaction?","a":"Platinum salt; hydrogen gas.","original":"Composition\n\nBASE PASTE                                 FUNCTION\n\nPolymethylhydrosiloxane                       Low molecular weight polymer\n\nFillers\n\nACCELERATOR PASTE\n\nDivinylpolysiloxane\n\nPlatinum Salt                                            Catalyst\n\nRetarder                                              Controls working and setting times\n\n Setting Reaction\n Vinyl siloxane + Silane siloxane                         Silicone rubber in presence of platinum salt\n\n •  Reaction activated by a platinum salt catalyst (chloroplatinic acid) without the release of by-products.\n •  In presence of impurites or moisture, secondary reaction takes place between the residual hydrides and\n    moisture leading to evolution of hydrogen gas"},{"p":38,"title":"PVS properties and pouring considerations","explanation":"Theory work 2-4 min, set 4-6.5 min, mix 45 sec. Pleasant odor/color, excellent detail, good tear strength (3000 gm/cm²), best dimensional stability, low curing shrinkage 0.17% and lowest permanent deformation 0.05-0.3%. Slide says delay stone pouring 1-2 h because hydrogen can cause pinpoint cast holes; some products include hydrogen scavengers. Extremely hydrophobic; surfactants may increase hydrophilicity. Shelf life 1-2 years. Lab timing and storage statements differ; follow product guidance in practice.","q":"Why may early PVS pouring create pinpoint holes in a cast?","a":"Hydrogen gas from a secondary reaction.","original":"Properties\n\n• Working time= 2-4 min\n• Setting time=4-6.5min\n• Mixing time of 45 secs\n• Pleasant odor and color\n• Excellent reproduction of surface details\n• Good tear strength (3000gm / cm2)\n• Best dimensional stability\n\n     - low curing shrinkage (0.17 %)\n\n     - lowest permanent deformation (0.05 - 0.3 %)\n• Stone pouring delayed by 1-2 hours\n•  Hydrogen gas by- product result in pinpoint holes in cast when poured immediately\n• Extremely hydrophobic, some manufacturers add a surfactant (detergent) to make it more\n  hydrophilic\n• Good shelf life of 1-2 yrs"},{"p":39,"title":"PVS advantages and latex warning","explanation":"Advantages: high accuracy, pleasant handling, short set, excellent elastic recovery, no main reaction by-product, stable dimensions, electroplating with silver/copper and multiple die pours. Disadvantages: shorter work time, possible hydrogen gas, hydrophobic, latex gloves interfere with setting. Vinyl/nitrile compatible gloves are alternatives. Do not interpret no main by-product as never releasing hydrogen.","q":"Which glove materials are listed as alternatives to latex?","a":"Vinyl and nitrile.","original":"Disadvantages  Advantages\n\n• Records highly accurate impression.     •  Shorter working time.\n• Pleasant to handle.                        •  May release Hydrogen gas on\n• Shorter setting time.                        setting.\n                                                •  Hydrophobic material.• Excellent elastic recovery.\n                                                •  Latex glove interfer with setting• No elimination of any by products.\n                                              reaction.Vinyl and nitrile gloves can• Dimensionally stable.\n                                    be used\n• Can be electroplated with silver &\n  copper.\n• Multiple die pour is possible."},{"p":40,"title":"Polyether forms and two polymerization types","explanation":"Two-paste system in low, medium and high consistencies; brand Impregum. Two types in source: ring-opening polymerization of aziridine groups; acid-catalyzed condensation polymerization of polyether prepolymer with alkoxysilane terminal groups. The next slide focuses on ring-opening reaction. Recognize the original cartridge/tube packaging.","q":"What two polymerization types does the polyether slide list?","a":"Aziridine ring-opening and acid-catalyzed condensation of alkoxysilane-ended polyether.","original":"Polyether\n\n• Supplied as two-paste system in low, medium and high consistencies\n• 2 types:\n    1. based on ring-opening polymerization of aziridine group\n    2. based on an acid-catalyzed condensation polymerization of\npolyether prepolymer with alkoxysilane terminal groups\n• Brands- IMPREGUM"},{"p":41,"title":"Polyether composition and setting","explanation":"Base: polyether polymer, colloidal silica filler, glycol ether or phthalate plasticizer. Catalyst: aromatic sulfonic acid ester enhances polymerization/cross-linking, colloidal silica filler and glycol ether/phthalate plasticizer. Ring-opening addition polymerization of imine-terminated polyether with aromatic sulfonate, no reaction by-products formed in this reaction. Cross-linking produces a stiff elastic network.","q":"What catalyst initiates the ring-opening polyether reaction?","a":"Aromatic sulfonic acid ester/sulfonate.","original":"Composition\n\n BASE PASTE                                 FUNCTION\n\n Polyether polymer\n\n Colloidal Silica                                                    Filler\n\n Glycol ether or phthalate                                   Plasticizer\n\n CATALYST PASTE\n\n Aromatic sulfonic acid ester                        Enhances further polymerisation and cross-linking\n\n Colloidal silica                                                    Filler\n\n Glycol ether or phthalate                                   Plasticizer\n\n\nSetting Reaction:\n•  Sets in ring opening addition polymerisation reaction of imine- terminated polyether with aromatic\n   sulfonate.\n• No reaction by-products are formed"},{"p":42,"title":"Polyether properties","explanation":"Theory work 3 min, set 6 min, mix 30 sec. Pleasant odor/taste, very good dimensional stability, curing shrinkage 0.24%, permanent deformation 1-2%. Very stiff (flexibility 3%); slide says about 4 mm extra space. Hydrophilic; moisture control less critical, shelf life up to 2 years. Source describes least biocompatible/highest cell cytotoxicity among compared materials. Lab p.19 labels a conflicting mixing time; do not silently substitute.","q":"What are the key handling limitations despite polyether's accuracy?","a":"Very stiff (3% flexibility), short work time, possible hypersensitivity; use appropriate spacing.","original":"Properties\n• Working time=3 min\n• Setting time= 6 min\n• Pleasant odor and taste\n• Mixing time is 30 secs\n• Dimensional stability is very good.\n• Curing shrinkage is low (0.24%)\n• The permanent deformation is also low (1-2%).\n• Very stiff (flexibility of 3%), needs extra space, around 4 mm is given.\n• Hydrophilic (moisture control not critical)\n• Shelf life extends upto 2 years\n• LEAST biocompatible - highest cell cytotoxicity"},{"p":43,"title":"Polyether trade-offs","explanation":"Advantages: good dimensional stability, accuracy, shorter set and good shelf life. Disadvantages: very stiff set material, imbibition, short work time and possible allergic hypersensitivity. Hydrophilicity can help wetting, but water uptake can change dimensions. Good accuracy does not mean perfect storage in water.","q":"Why does hydrophilicity not guarantee dimensional stability in water?","a":"Imbibition can cause swelling and dimensional changes.","original":"Advantages\n\n• Good Dimensional stability\n• Accuracy\n• Shorter setting time\n• Good Shelf life\n\nDisadvantages:\n• Set material very stiff\n• Imbibition\n• Short working time\n• Allergic hypersensitivity in some cases."},{"p":44,"title":"Double-mix single impression","explanation":"The original multiple-mix technique combines light-body syringe material around the prepared tooth with heavy-body tray material. The heavy body supports the detail-recording light body. Both are used in one impression. The photo sequence shows tray, syringe, dispensing and completed impression. Compare the single-step lab putty-wash sequence.","q":"Which two consistencies are paired in double mix single impression?","a":"Light body syringe material and heavy body tray material.","original":"Techniques:\n• MULTIPLE MIX/ DOUBLE MIX SINGLE IMPRESSION\n-Syringe Material (LIGHT BODY) + Tray Material (HEAVY BODY)"},{"p":45,"title":"Two-step putty-wash technique","explanation":"The source labels putty plus light body. A putty impression is first created, then a wash adds fine detail; the dedicated lab source p.25 gives the explicit six-step sequence with a separator. The original photos show mixed putty, spacer arrangement and added wash. Unlike the double-mix method, putty and wash are not set at the same time.","q":"What distinguishes two-step putty wash from double-mix single impression?","a":"Putty is set first, then light-body wash is used in a second impression.","original":"2 Step Putty Wash\n\n• PUTTY + LIGHT BODY"},{"p":46,"title":"Complete elastomer comparison table","explanation":"The original image compares polysulfide, condensation silicone, addition silicone, polyether in that order. Work min: 4-7, 2.5-4, 2-4, 3. Set min: 7-10, 6-8, 4-6.5, 6. Tear strength N/m: 2500-7000, 2300-2600, 1500-4300, 1800-4800. Contraction at 24 h %: 0.40-0.45, 0.38-0.60, 0.14-0.17, 0.19-0.24. Water contact angle: 82°, 98°, 98/53° (hydrophilized PVS), 49°. Hydrogen gas: N,N,Y,N (absorber often included). Automatic mixing N,N,Y,Y; custom tray Y,N,N,N; unpleasant odor Y,N,N,N; multiple casts N,N,Y,Y. Stiffness ranking (1 greatest): 3, 2(1), 2(1), 1(2), parentheses for soft polyether formulations. Removal distortion rank (1 greatest, 4 least): 1,2,4,3. These table values must be read as table figures; some individual slides and lab figures differ.","q":"Which material has lowest removal distortion ranking and which is stiffest in the original table?","a":"PVS (4 = least distortion); conventional polyether (1 = greatest stiffness).","original":""},{"p":47,"title":"Disinfection: preserve the lecture comparison","explanation":"The original table states alginate: cautious short immersion (<10 min), chlorine compounds or iodophors; short glutaraldehyde exposure inadequate for disinfection. Agar: do not immerse in alkaline glutaraldehyde. Polysulfide/silicone: immersion, glutaraldehydes, chlorine compounds, iodophors, phenolics; >30 min exposures not recommended. Polyether: cautious short immersion (<10 min), chlorine compounds or iodophors; short exposure avoids distortion. ZOE paste: immersion preferred, spray for bite registrations, glutaraldehydes or iodophors; not chlorine, phenolic spray possible. Compound: iodophors/chlorine, phenolic spray possible. This is a historical lecture table, not an approved disinfection procedure; use validated instructions and product compatibility for real work.","q":"Why does the lecture advise caution with alginate and polyether immersion?","a":"Water exposure can distort these materials; use compatible validated disinfection conditions.","original":"Disinfection"},{"p":48,"title":"Conclusion: material selection is a balance","explanation":"An ideal impression material balances accuracy, dimensional stability, handling and comfort. No single material is perfect for every situation. Addition silicone and polyether offer excellent accuracy and stability; alginate is comparatively less stable and accurate. Selection depends on patient therapeutic needs. For exams, explain both the advantage and the trade-off of each material.","q":"Which two elastomers are emphasized for accuracy and dimensional stability?","a":"Addition silicone (PVS) and polyether.","original":"CONCLUSION\n\n• An ideal impression material balances accuracy,dimensional\n  stability,handling properties and patient comfort\n• No Single impression material is perfect for all situations\n• Addition silicone and polyether offer excellent accuracy and\n dimensional stability.\n• Whereas Alginates are less dimensionally stable and accurate.\n• Clinicians must select the impression material according to the\n  patient’s therapeutic need."},{"p":49,"title":"References and further reading","explanation":"The lecture cites Philips' Science of Dental Materials (Anusavice, Shen, Rawls, 12th edition), Craig's Restorative Dental Materials (Sakaguchi, Ferracane, Powers, 14th edition), and Skinner's Science of Dental Materials (Ralph W. Phillips, 7th edition). The original reference slide is retained; the current companion is based on the supplied lecture, not a replacement textbook. Review the material families, tables, chemistry and equipment in the final flashcards.","q":"Which three textbook titles are listed in the source?","a":"Philips' Science of Dental Materials, Craig's Restorative Dental Materials, Skinner's Science of Dental Materials.","original":"References\n• Anusavice,Shen,Rawls: Philips’ Science of Dental Materials,12th\n  Edition\n• Ronald Sakaguchi,Jack Ferracane, John Powers:Craig’s Restorative\n Dental Materials , 14th Edition\n• Ralph W.Phillips ; Seventh edition ; Skinner’s science of Dental\n  Materials"}]},"lab-impression":{"title":"Impression Materials","file":"4_Impression materials lab _AC.pdf","pages":25,"groups":[[1,3,"Introduction & classification"],[4,8,"Plaster & compound"],[9,11,"ZOE & impression wax"],[12,13,"Agar & alginate"],[14,16,"Handling, setting & water"],[17,19,"Polysulfide & polyether"],[20,23,"Silicones & body grades"],[24,25,"One-step & two-step"]],"guide":[{"p":1,"title":"Impression materials: laboratory orientation","explanation":"This handout is your laboratory reference, taught by Dr. Lovely with Dr. Adla Ahmed. The practical goal is to recognize each material, classify it, explain what it is used for, and recall its handling and timing. Learn the images as well as the words; a tray, powder packet, two tubes, cake or stick may appear in a practical identification station.","q":"What are the four main things to learn for each impression material?","a":"Identification, classification, use, and handling/properties.","original":"IMPRESSION MATERIALS\n\nINSTRUCTOR: DR. LOVELY\n\nGP INCHARGE: DR. ADLA AHMED"},{"p":2,"title":"What a good impression does","explanation":"An impression material records the shapes of teeth and their soft supporting tissues. It creates a negative copy; the positive replica is produced when cast material fills that negative. A suitable material should be non-toxic and non-irritating (biocompatible), flow sufficiently to record the surface, and be easy to manipulate. After setting, it should keep the recorded dimensions. Price and availability, shelf life, acceptable setting time, and pleasant taste, odor and color also matter. Think of a good impression as an accurate mold that can be removed without losing its shape.","q":"Name the eight ideal property groups.","a":"Biocompatibility, flow, manipulation, stability, price/availability, shelf life, setting time, pleasant sensory properties.","original":"What is IMPRESSION MATERIAL?\n\n•    It is the material which is used to take an impression of teeth and soft supportive tissues.\n\n\nIdeal properties of Impression Material :\n   Non-toxic and non-irritant (Biocompatible).•\n  Good flow.•\n   Easy manipulation.•\n   Dimensionally stable after setting.•\n   Acceptable price and availability.•\n  Good shelf life.•\n   Acceptable setting time.•\n   Pleasant taste, odor, and color.•"},{"p":3,"title":"The material family tree","explanation":"First ask whether the set material can flex. Non-elastic materials are impression plaster, compound, zinc oxide eugenol (ZOE) and impression waxes. They cannot easily bend around undercuts. Elastic materials divide into water-based hydrocolloids and non-aqueous elastomers. Hydrocolloids are reversible agar and irreversible alginate. Elastomers are polysulfide, polyether, condensation silicone and addition silicone. Reversible means heating/cooling changes the physical state; irreversible means a chemical setting process cannot simply be undone by heating. The original classification chart is shown alongside this explanation.","q":"Which two groups of elastic materials are in the lab chart?","a":"Hydrocolloids (agar, alginate) and non-aqueous elastomers (polysulfide, polyether, silicones).","original":""},{"p":4,"title":"Impression plaster and undercuts","explanation":"Impression plaster is an early dental impression material and is rigid after setting. The lab notes describe limited flow and use with mild or no undercuts in edentulous patients for final impressions. An undercut is a recessed region behind a wider contour: see the diagrams of proximal tooth undercuts and lingual soft-tissue or bony ridge undercuts. A rigid material can lock in such a region, then fracture or distort on removal. The product and the edentulous impression photographs help you identify it.","q":"Why is a rigid material unsuitable for deep undercuts?","a":"It cannot flex around the wider contour and may become locked or fracture.","original":"Non-elastic impression material:\n\n\n1- Impression Plaster\n\n   ▪Earliest impression material in dentistry.\n  ▪Rigid impression materials (it has limited flow).\n     Used in mild or no undercuts.\n  ▪Only used in edentulous patients (final impression)."},{"p":5,"title":"Plaster: use and handling facts","explanation":"The two uses on this slide are final impression of an edentulous patient and bite registration. Setting time is 3-5 minutes in this lab handout. Its main limitation is brittleness: it fractures easily. In the photographs the white plaster is used to register the relationship of the teeth. Remember that this is impression plaster, not the usual model or die stone used to pour a cast.","q":"What are the lab setting time and two uses of impression plaster?","a":"3-5 min; final edentulous impression and bite registration.","original":"Uses of impression plaster :\n\n\n1-Final impression of edentulous patient.\n2-Bite registration.\n\n\nProperties :\n\n* Setting time 3–5 minutes.\n*Brittle and fractures easily."},{"p":6,"title":"Impression compound: ingredients and purpose","explanation":"Compound is rigid after cooling but reversible because its setting is physical, not a chemical reaction. It is thermoplastic: heat softens it, cooling hardens it. Uses are preliminary edentulous impressions, special trays and border molding. The original formulation is expressed in parts: resin 30, copal resin 30, carnauba wax 10, stearic acid 5, talc 25, plus coloring agents. Resin and wax contribute the softening behavior; talc is a filler and stearic acid aids manipulation.","q":"What are the five stated ingredient proportions in impression compound?","a":"Resin 30, copal 30, carnauba 10, stearic acid 5, talc 25 parts.","original":"2- Impression Compound\n\n    •  One of the oldest impression materials.\n        Rigid, Reversible.\n    •  Thermoplastic (set by physical\n       changes).\nUses of Impression Compound:\n\n    •  Preliminary impression of edentulous\n        patient. Special tray.\n    •  Border molding.                                               Composition\n\n                                        Ingredients                                   Parts\n\n                                              Resin                              30\n\n                                   Copal resin                            30\n\n                                 Carnauba wax                           10\n\n                                             Stearic acid                           5\n                                                   Talc                              25\n\n                                                             Coloring agents"},{"p":7,"title":"Recognize brown cakes and green sticks","explanation":"In this lab lecture, the photograph identifies Type I as a brown cake and Type II as a green stick. A cake is a broad piece used to record the general shape of an edentulous arch. The green stick is used at the edge of a custom tray during border molding. Border molding records the shape and functional extension of the peripheral tissues. Important: the separate theory lecture uses a different Type I/II classification based on fusing temperature. Learn the form and use as well as the lab's stated labels, and confirm which terminology your instructor expects.","q":"According to this lab slide, identify the two forms.","a":"Type I brown cake; Type II green stick (lab-specific labels).","original":"Impression\ncompound\n\n\n\n\n\n      Type I\n    Brown cake\n\n\n\n\n\n      Type II\n    Green stick"},{"p":8,"title":"Compound: heat, appearance and application","explanation":"Brown cake: non-elastic, primary impression with poor fine detail, used for edentulous patients with mild/no undercut; soften by heat and harden by cooling. Green stick: non-elastic, used to border mold a custom tray, softened with a flame in the demonstration. A soft stick looks glossy; a hard one is matte. Overheating may cause oil loss. The listed components are wax, natural resin, filler (adds viscosity and rigidity), pigment (color), and stearic acid (lubricant). The slide gives softening at 45-60 degrees C maximum and hardening at 37 degrees C. These are lecture facts, not a substitute for the specific product instructions or supervised handling.","q":"What visual clue distinguishes softened from hardened green stick compound?","a":"Glossy when soft, matte when hard; overheating can cause oil loss.","original":"Type I : Brown cake impression compound non-elastic impression material\n   Used to takea Primaryimpression ( poor details).•\n   Softened with heat, harden with cooling.•\n   Used for edentulous patients with mild or no undercut.•\n\n\nType II : Green stick impression compound non-elastic impression material\n   Border molding ofcustom tray.•\n   Softened with flame.•\n   Glossy when its soft, matt when its hard ( over heat will cause oil loss ).•\n\n\n\nImpression compound component :\n• Wax\n• Natural resin\n• Filler and pigments (fillers give viscosity which makes it rigid / pigment gives color).\n•Stearic acid: as a lubricant material to make it to work with.\n• Impression compound become Soft at 45 - 60 max and Hard at 37 degree C."},{"p":9,"title":"Zinc oxide eugenol impression paste","explanation":"ZOE is a non-elastic two-tube material. Identify the white zinc oxide base and colored eugenol catalyst. It is used for secondary/final edentulous impressions without undercuts, mixed on a glass slab, and poured with stone rather than plaster according to the lab. It is thin and brittle, so handle carefully. The slide also lists sedative effect, temporary filling and bite registration. Advantages: cheap, accurate detail and dimensional stability. Disadvantages: possible irritation/allergy, thin/brittle/sticky material and 5-15 min setting time. Distinguish ZOE impression paste from the cement formulations in Lab 5.","q":"Which tube is white, what are the main uses, and why avoid undercuts?","a":"Zinc oxide base; final edentulous impression, temporary filling, bite registration; brittle material can break.","original":"3- Zinc Oxide Eugenol ( ZOE ) non-elastic impression material\n\n   Comes in 2 tubes:\n                 1-Base ( zinc oxide ) , white\n                   2Catalyst ( eugenol ) , colored\n\n\n    •  Used for Secondary/final impression for edentulous patient.\n    •    It have Sedative effect.\n    •  Very thin and brittle “handle with care after taking an impression”.\n     Used in patients with no undercuts (otherwise it will break ).\n    •    It’s poured with stone not plaster.\n    •    It’s mixed on a glass slab.\n    •  Used as temporary filling.\n    •  Used as bite registration material."},{"p":10,"title":"Non-eugenol alternatives","explanation":"Eugenol may cause a burning sensation. Non-eugenol pastes were developed for patients irritated by eugenol, and the slide notes that bactericides and other medicaments may be incorporated. The product photograph shows a two-paste alternative. The distinction is about ingredients and tolerance, not whether the material has suddenly become elastic.","q":"Why use non-eugenol impression paste?","a":"To avoid burning or irritation caused by eugenol.","original":""},{"p":11,"title":"Impression waxes","explanation":"These are non-elastic thermoplastic materials that flow at mouth temperature and are soft at room temperature; they do not set through a chemical reaction. The slide describes low-melting paraffin wax and beeswax in approximately a 3:1 ratio. They correct small imperfections such as air blows in other impressions, particularly ZOE impressions. Pour a cast immediately to avoid distortion of wax. The lecture notes that impression wax is not commonly used. Compare these with the corrective and bite registration waxes in Lab 6.","q":"Why must an impression wax be poured immediately?","a":"Wax readily distorts; immediate pouring helps preserve dimensions.","original":"4- Impression waxes non-elastic impression material"},{"p":12,"title":"Agar: reversible hydrocolloid","explanation":"Agar is an organic hydrocolloid extracted from certain seaweeds. Temperature changes can reversibly convert its physical state. It is used for final impressions and is supplied as syringe material and tray material. The listed ingredients are agar, borax, potassium sulfate, benzoate and additives. The lab says it can be reused because it is reversible; this means physical reconditioning, not permission to reuse contaminated patient material. Follow infection-control protocols in actual practice.","q":"Which elastic hydrocolloid is reversible and what are its two forms?","a":"Agar; syringe material and tray material.","original":"Elastic impression material:\n\n\n1.1-Agar reversible Hydrocolloid elastic impression material 1.2-\nAlginate irreversible Hydrocolloid elastic impression material\n\n\n\n\n1.1-Agar reversible Hydrocolloid elastic impression material\nIt is an organic hydrocolloid extracted from certain types of seaweeds.\nIts reversible material, so it could be reused (change with heat).\nUsed to take final impression.\nSupplied as 2 forms:\n1-Syrings\n2-Tray material\n\nComposition:\n1. Agar\n2.  Borax\n3.  Potassium sulphate\n4.  Benzoate\n5.  Additives"},{"p":13,"title":"Alginate: composition, types and timing","explanation":"Alginate is an irreversible elastic hydrocolloid that replaced agar in routine use. It is used for primary impressions, including undercuts; the lab describes relatively poor detail compared with precision impressions. It comes in different colors. Ingredient table: potassium/sodium/triethanolamine alginate 15% (dissolves and reacts with calcium); calcium sulfate dihydrate 16% (forms insoluble calcium alginate gel); zinc oxide 4% (filler, affects properties/set); potassium titanium fluoride 3% (counteracts inhibition of stone setting and improves cast surface); diatomaceous earth 60% (filler, controls consistency and flexibility); trisodium phosphate 2% (retarder, regulates setting); coloring and flavoring agents. Normal-set: mix 60 sec, work 2 min, set 2-5 min. Fast-set: mix 30-45 sec, work 1-1.5 min, set 1-2 min. Learn the times by separating mixing from working and setting.","q":"Compare normal and fast alginate mixing, working and setting times.","a":"Normal 60 s / 2 min / 2-5 min; fast 30-45 s / 1-1.5 min / 1-2 min.","original":"1.2-Alginate irreversible Hydrocolloid elastic impression material It\nis an irreversible impression material .\nReplaced Agar recently.\nUsed for primary impression (poor details).\nUsed with undercut.\nComes in different colors.                    Composition\n\n2 types ofalginate:\nNormal-setting alginate\nSetting time : 2 - 5 minutes.\nWorking time : 2 minutes.\nMixing time : 60 sec.\nFast-setting alginate\nSetting time: 1 - 2 minutes.\nWorking time : 1-1.5 minutes.\nmixing time : 30-45 sec."},{"p":14,"title":"Alginate: controlling the set and taking an impression","explanation":"The lab notes say more powder relative to water shortens setting time and less powder delays it. Cold water lengthens the set; warm water shortens it. In practice use the specified powder/water ratio and product temperature instructions rather than deliberately altering proportions. The material is set when rubbery; some products indicate the stage by a color change. The illustrated sequence is: choose a perforated tray, mix and load, make the impression, wait (the example says 3 min), remove rapidly in one movement, then trim excess. The lab says pour within 30 min and warns of distortion after more than 24 h. Actual timing depends on the product; the 3-minute photograph is an example, not the fast-set specification.","q":"How do cold and warm water affect setting? When should the cast be poured according to the lab?","a":"Cold slows, warm accelerates; within 30 min.","original":"CONTROLLING SETTING TIME\n\n1. Powder / water ratio :\n• More powder >> decreases setting time (sets faster)\n• Less powder >> increases setting time (delayed setting)\n\n2. Water temperature :\n• Cold water >> increases setting time\n• Warm water >> decreases setting time\n\nHow to know that the alginate is set?\n- When it becomes rubbery.\n- Some alginates change color when it set.\n\n\n*You have to poor the impression within 30 min.\n*If left more than 24 hours the dimension will be\n distort."},{"p":15,"title":"Syneresis, imbibition and disinfection","explanation":"Syneresis means loss of water, so the impression dries and shrinks. Imbibition means absorption of water, so the impression swells. Both change the dimensions and explain why alginate must be handled promptly. Advantages: elastic, cheap, good taste, easy mixing/manipulation, patient comfort, hygienic. Disadvantages: relatively poor detail, tears easily, poor dimensional stability. Disinfection agents named: iodophors, sodium hypochlorite, glutaraldehyde, phenyl phenol. These are lecture-listed categories; clinical selection, concentration and contact time require a compatible product and infection-control protocol.","q":"Define syneresis and imbibition.","a":"Syneresis: water loss and shrinkage; imbibition: water absorption and expansion.","original":"• Syneresis : impression dry out and shrink.\n• Imbibition : impression soak water and expand.\n\n\n\n                  Advantages                          Disadvantages\n\n         •   Elastic material.                              •  Poor details production.\n         •  Cheap material.                              •  Tears easily.\n         •  Good taste.                                  •  Poor dimension stability.\n         •  Easy to mix & manipulate.\n         •  Comfortable to patient.\n         •  Hygienic.\n\n\nDisinfection of impressions:\n\n• Iodophors\n• Sodium hypochlorite\n• Glutaraldehyde\n• Phenyl phenol"},{"p":16,"title":"Product recognition: normal and fast alginate","explanation":"The photographs show Cavex CA37 normal-set dust-free alginate and Cavex ColorChange fast-set dust-free alginate. Product appearance varies, so read the package as well as observing the color. A color-change product may indicate a stage of mixing or setting. In an identification exam, state alginate, irreversible elastic hydrocolloid, normal or fast type if indicated, and its primary impression use.","q":"What product features distinguish these two photographed alginates?","a":"Cavex CA37 normal-set; Cavex ColorChange fast-set.","original":""},{"p":17,"title":"Polysulfide: form, viscosity and timing","explanation":"Polysulfide is a synthetic elastic elastomer. Supplied as two pastes: white base and gray or brown accelerator. Three viscosities: light, medium and heavy body. Lab working time 5-7 min; setting time 8-12 min; pour the impression within 30-60 min. Working time is the period in which the material can still be handled; setting time is the period until it sets. Theory slide values differ, so use the lab figures for this particular lab lecture.","q":"Recall the polysulfide lab timing trio.","a":"Work 5-7 min, set 8-12 min, pour 30-60 min.","original":"2.1-Polysulfide elastomer elastic impression material\n2.2-Polyether elastomer elastic impression material 2.3-\nSillicone elastomer elastic impression material\n\n2.1-Polysulfide elastomer elastic impression material It\nis a synthetic elastomeric impression material. Supplied\nas 2 paste system :\n Base : white.\n Accelerator : gray or brown.\n\nAvailable in 3 viscosities :\n1-Light bodies.\n2-Medium bodies.\n3-Heavy bodies.\n\nWorking time : 5-7 minutes.\nSetting time : 8-12 minutes.\nImpression pouring : 30-60 minutes."},{"p":18,"title":"Polysulfide: identify advantages and limitations","explanation":"The brown impression and two-paste package in the slide are practical clues. Polysulfide is a secondary impression material. Advantages: good flexibility, tear resistance and high surface detail. Disadvantages: long setting time, unpleasant odor and poor dimensional stability. Link these facts: the material is useful for detailed recording and removal, but an impression may change shape if stored too long.","q":"What are three advantages and three disadvantages of polysulfide?","a":"Flexibility, tear resistance, detail; long set, odor, poor dimensional stability.","original":"2.1-Polysulfide elastomer elastic impression material\n\n            Advantages                          Disadvantages\n\nGood flexibility                          Long setting time\nTear resistance                            Unpleasant odor\nHigh detail surface                         Poor dimensional stability\n\n\n                                     Secondary impression"},{"p":19,"title":"Polyether: accurate but stiff","explanation":"Elastic secondary impression material, supplied as base and accelerator in light, medium and heavy viscosities. The lab lists working time 2-5 min, a line labeled mixing time 4-5 min, and pouring up to one week of storage. Theory gives a different mixing time (30 sec), so the lab label is uncertain and should be confirmed. Advantages: easy mix/handling, accuracy, excellent detail, dimensional stability and use with undercuts. Disadvantages: possible allergy, rapid setting, expense and high stiffness. Its elasticity does not mean it is very flexible; stiffness makes removal from undercuts challenging.","q":"Which elastomer is accurate and dimensionally stable but very stiff?","a":"Polyether.","original":"2.2-Polyether elastomer elastic impression material\nIt is an elastic impression material used to take secondary impression.\nCome as Base and Accelerator.\nAvailable in 3 viscosities:\n1- Light bodies.\n2- Medium bodies.\n3- Heavy bodies.\n\nWorking time : 2-5 minutes\nMixing time : 4-5 minutes\nImpression pouring: up to 1 week of storage\n\n\n\n\n                Advantages                          Disadvantages\n\n     Easy to mix and handle                        Allergic reaction\n     High accuracy                           Rapid setting time\n      Excellent surface details                     Expensive\n     Dimensionally stable                      High stiffness\n    Good on undercuts"},{"p":20,"title":"Condensation silicone","explanation":"Silicones are elastic final impression materials used in stock or special trays. The slide lists two pastes or paste plus liquid: base provides body/thickness, catalyst accelerates the reaction. Condensation silicone comes as light, medium, heavy or putty. Lab work 3 min, set 6-8 min, pour within first 30 min. Advantages: excellent detail, short set, easy use, suitable for severe undercuts per slide. Disadvantages: hydrophobic, irritant, very strong odor per lab slide, and immediate pouring required. The theory notes attribute shrinkage to the alcohol by-product. The odor descriptions differ between sources; keep lab statements distinct.","q":"Why should condensation silicone be poured promptly?","a":"Loss of volatile by-product reduces dimensional stability; lab says within 30 min.","original":"2.3-Sillicone elastomer elastic impression material\n*It is an elastic impression material used with both stock and special trays to take final impression.\n*Supplied as :\n•  2 pastes OR paste and liquid (Base : give the material thickness and Catalyst : accelerate the reaction).\n\n2.3.1-Condensation Sillicone elastomer elastic impression material\n*Available as :\n1-Light body.\n2-Medium body.\n3-Heavy body.\n4-Very heavy body (putty).\n\nWorking time : 3 minutes\nSetting time : 6-8 minutes\nImpressoin poured : within first 30 minutes\n\n\n                Advantages                    Disadvantages\n\n         Excellent surface details             Hydrophobic\n        Short setting time                           Irritant\n       Easy to use                          Pour immediately\n       Used with severe undercut           Very strong odor"},{"p":21,"title":"Addition silicone (PVS)","explanation":"Available in low, medium, high and very high consistencies, and supplied in jars, cartridges, syringes and sausages. The lab states colored base and white catalyst. Working time 2-5 min, setting time 3-7 min, pouring up to one week. Advantages: dimensional stability, excellent detail, non-toxic/non-irritant, easy use and fast set. Disadvantages: expensive, short working time, hydrophobic, and latex can interfere with polymerization. In clinical handling use compatible gloves (such as nitrile) rather than removing protective gloves. Theory lists other timing ranges; keep both lecture contexts separate.","q":"What is the important glove-related warning for PVS?","a":"Latex can inhibit setting; use compatible gloves such as nitrile.","original":"2.3.2-Additional Sillicone elastomer elastic impression material\n   *Available as : low, medium, high, and very high consistencies.\n   *Supplied as : jars, cartridge, syringes, and sausages.\n   *The base is colored while the catalyst is white in color.\n\n\n   Working time : 2-5 minutes Setting\n   time : 3-7 minutes Impression\n   poured : up to 1 week\n\n\n\n         Advantages                    Disadvantages\n\nDimensionally     stable             Expensive\nExcellent surface detail              Short working time\nNon  toxic,  non  irritant              Can’t use it with latex\nEasy to use                                  Hydrophobic\nFast setting"},{"p":22,"title":"Body grades: what the different consistencies do","explanation":"Light body flows into fine features and is usually combined with another PVS; used for final crown and bridge impressions. Medium body can be used alone or in combination for final impressions. Heavy body can be used alone or in combination, providing bulk and support. Putty is very heavy: used for primary impressions or combined with another silicone for final impressions, and sometimes for complete denture border molding instead of green stick. The photo examples show light, monophase, heavy and putty packaging. Avoid latex interference using compatible gloves.","q":"Which PVS body records fine detail, and which provides bulk or border molding?","a":"Light body records detail; putty provides bulk and can be used for border molding.","original":"1.Light body additional silicone elastomer elastic impression material -\nUsually used in combination with another additional silicone\n-Used to take final impressions for crowns and bridges\n\n2.Medium body additional silicone elastomer elastic impression material\nCan be used alone or in combination to take final impressions\n\n3.Heavy body additional silicone elastomer elastic impression material\nCan be used alone or in combination\n\n\n\n\n\n4.Putty additional silicone elastic impression material\nUsed to take a primary impression or in combination with another silicone for final impression.\nUsed for border molding in complete denture patients {sometimes instead of greenstick compound}. Latex\nin gloves affects the polymerization of the material {gloves should not be worn while mixing the product\nor use nitrile gloves}."},{"p":23,"title":"Recognize the trays, automix system and finished impressions","explanation":"The photographs illustrate an automix dispensing gun and syringe, a tray with material, upper and lower arch impressions, and multiple two-color impressions. A higher-viscosity material supports the more flowing material, which reaches fine detail. Color is product-dependent, so do not identify a material purely by its color. Distinguish an upper arch with palatal coverage from the open horseshoe of a lower arch.","q":"What do the two contrasting colors in a putty-wash impression usually represent?","a":"Two viscosities: supporting putty/heavier body and detail-recording wash.","original":""},{"p":24,"title":"One-step impression technique","explanation":"The one-step method combines putty and light body at the same time. Mix putty base and catalyst and adapt it to the tray; immediately inject light body around the prepared tooth or teeth; then take the impression. Both materials polymerize simultaneously. The image sequence shows mixing, loading the tray, applying light body and the finished two-material impression. Remember simultaneous setting is the defining feature.","q":"What makes the one-step technique one step?","a":"Putty and light body polymerize simultaneously in one impression.","original":"One step technique\n1.Mix putty(base& catalyst) and adapt on tray.\n2.Immediately inject light body around prepared tooth or teeth.\n3.Take an impression {both materials polymerize simultaneously}."},{"p":25,"title":"Two-step impression technique","explanation":"First mix putty base and catalyst, adapt it to the tray and place a separator on top. Take the first impression. After it sets, remove it and remove the separator, leaving room for the wash. Inject light body over the set putty and take the impression again. Wait until the second material sets, then remove. Compare the photographed blue putty and yellow wash. The first material is already set when the second is added, unlike the one-step method. This sequence is for studying the instructor's lab demonstration; actual clinical performance requires supervision.","q":"What is the purpose of the separator in the two-step method?","a":"It creates space for the light-body wash after the putty sets.","original":"Two step impression technique\n1.Mix putty (base &catalyst).\n2. Adapt on a tray and place separator on top.\n3.Take an impression.\n4.After the impression is set remove it from the patient mouth and remove the separator.\n5.Inject light body over the set putty impression and retake the impression again.\n6.Wait until the impression sets and then remove it."}]},"lab-cements":{"title":"Dental Cements","file":"5_Cements lab _AC.pdf","pages":13,"groups":[[1,3,"Foundations & classification"],[4,5,"Permanent luting cements"],[6,7,"Temporary cements"],[8,9,"Liners, bases & Dycal"],[10,11,"GIC & RMGIC ratios"],[12,13,"Biodentine & equipment"]],"guide":[{"p":1,"title":"Dental cements: laboratory orientation","explanation":"This lab by Dr. Lovely and Dr. Adla Ahmed focuses on identifying cement packages and equipment, knowing where each material goes, and recalling the correct lecture-specific ratios and preparation sequence. Distinguish a cement used to lute a crown from a base or liner that protects the pulp.","q":"What are the three principal tasks in this cement lab?","a":"Identification, material selection by use, and mixing/dispensing recall.","original":"DENTAL CEMENTS\n\n\nINSTRUCTOR: DR. LOVELY\n\nGP INCHARGE: DR. ADLA AHMED"},{"p":2,"title":"What a dental cement is","explanation":"A cement begins in a viscous state and hardens into a solid, joining two surfaces. It can act as a base, liner, filling material or luting cement that binds a prosthesis to a tooth. Delivery forms: powder and liquid, base and catalyst paste, or premixed capsule. Ideal requirements: non-toxic and non-irritating to pulp, insoluble, adequate mechanical properties, adhesion to enamel and dentin, bacteriostatic behavior, appropriate optical properties, and thermal, chemical and electrical insulation. The crown image shows luting: the cement lies between the restoration and prepared tooth.","q":"Name the four roles of a dental cement.","a":"Base, liner, filling material, luting cement.","original":"What is DENTAL CEMENT ?\nA substance that harden from a viscous state to a solid state to join 2 surfaces.\nA cement acts as base, liner, filling material or luting cement to bind prosthesis to tooth surface.\n\n\nAvailable as\n  ➢Powder➢       and liquid.:\n  ➢Base and catalyst paste.\n  ➢Premixed capsules.\n\n\nIdeal Requirements:\n   Nontoxic, nonirritant to pulp tissue.•\n•  Insoluble.\n•  Mechanical properties.\n•  Adhesion to enamel and dentin.\n•  Bacteriostatic.\n•  Optical properties.\n•  Thermal, chemical and electrical insulation."},{"p":3,"title":"Classify luting cements","explanation":"The lab flowchart divides permanent luting into self-cure luting GIC and resin-based cements; resin cements are adhesive or self-adhesive. Temporary luting includes polymer-reinforced ZOE restorative cement (IRM), ZOE and zinc oxide non-eugenol. The smaller table also classifies by matrix: water-based (glass and resin-modified glass ionomer, zinc polycarboxylate, zinc phosphate), oil-based (ZOE and non-eugenol zinc oxide), resin-based (composite and adhesive resins, compomer). The same lecture later labels IRM permanent; this is an internal contradiction, not a fact to memorize without qualification.","q":"What are the two branches of resin-based permanent luting cement?","a":"Adhesive resin cement and self-adhesive resin cement.","original":"Self cure luting GIC\n                                                                                Adhesive resin\n                         Permanent Luting                                         cement\n                              cements\n                                                              Resin basedLuting cements                                                        cements                                                                                    Self-adhesive resin\n                                                                                 cement\n\n\n\n\n                                                   Polymer reinforced\n                                         ZOE restorative\n                                                   cement ( IRM )\n\n                        Temporary Luting\n                               cements                                                           Zinc oxide eugenol\n\n\n\n                                                             Zinc oxide non-\n                                                        eugenol"},{"p":4,"title":"Self-cure GIC for permanent luting","explanation":"Self-cure glass ionomer luting cement comes as powder and liquid or a premixed capsule. The lecture's powder/liquid proportion is one scoop powder to two drops liquid, mixed on a glass slab with a cement spatula. The capsule is mechanically mixed using an amalgamator and dispensed with an applicator; later slides clarify that capsule activation precedes mixing. The photographs show bottles, spatulated cement on glass, Ketac Cem Aplicap packaging and a capsule. Ratios are formulation-specific in real practice.","q":"What is the self-cure luting GIC ratio and capsule workflow?","a":"1 scoop powder : 2 drops liquid; activate, amalgamate, dispense with applicator.","original":"Self cure GIC permanent luting cement\nAvailable as :\n\n\n   Powder and liquid•\n -Mixed in a ratio of 1 scoop powder: 2 drop liquid on a glass slab with a cement spatula.\n   Premixed in a capsule•\n  -Activated in an amalgamator.\n  -Dispensed using in applicator."},{"p":5,"title":"Adhesive vs self-adhesive resin cement","explanation":"Adhesive resin cements typically need a separate primer for bonding to the tooth and to alloy or ceramic substrates. The lecture states they bond most alloy and ceramic restorations except veneers; this is the handout's framing, not a universal clinical rule for all adhesive products. Self-adhesive resin cement removes the separate etch, prime and bond steps as listed on the slide. Compare the dual-barrel syringe and the packaged resin cement system. The main exam difference is the separate conditioning/priming procedure.","q":"What steps does the lecture say self-adhesive resin cement eliminates?","a":"Separate etching, priming and bonding.","original":"Adhesive resin permanent luting cement\nThese cements typically require a separate primer for\n•  bonding to tooth\n•  Alloy/ceramic substrates.\nUsed for bonding of most alloy and ceramic restorations, except veneers.\n\n\n\nSelf-Adhesive resin permanent luting cement\nThe steps of etching, priming, and bonding are eliminated."},{"p":6,"title":"IRM: intermediate restoration material","explanation":"The slide places IRM under temporary luting and describes it as an intermediate restoration intended for no longer than one year. It is polymer-reinforced zinc oxide eugenol. It can serve as a base beneath restorative materials and cements that do not contain resin components. Initial set is about 5 min from the beginning of mixing, with a 1:1 powder/liquid ratio in this lecture. The photo shows IRM powder/liquid and a temporary bridge example. Later slides conflict by calling it permanent luting; retain that discrepancy separately.","q":"What does IRM contain, how long is it intended to remain, and what ratio is listed?","a":"Polymer-reinforced ZOE; no longer than 1 year; 1:1 powder/liquid.","original":"IRM temporary luting cement\nIt is an intermediate restoration materialintended to remain in place for no longer than one year. It is also\nbe used as a base under restorative materials and cements that do not contain resin components.\n\nComponents:\nPolymer reinforced zinc oxide eugenol.\n\nInitial set occurs approximately 5 minutes since the mixing started.\n\nMixing ratio :\n1 to 1 (powder/liquid)"},{"p":7,"title":"Temporary zinc oxide cements","explanation":"ZOE temporary luting cement is shown as powder and liquid mixed on a glass slab. Zinc oxide non-eugenol cement is shown as base and catalyst pastes. Look for the packaging and delivery forms in the photographs: bottles compared with two tubes. Non-eugenol is a different formulation, not simply a different color of the same material.","q":"Distinguish the two temporary cement presentations on slide 7.","a":"ZOE powder/liquid; non-eugenol base/catalyst paste.","original":"Zinc oxide eugenol (Temporary luting cement) Zinc oxide non-eugenol\n powder and liquid mixed on a glass                              base and a catalyst paste\nslab"},{"p":8,"title":"Liner, varnish and base: understand the layers","explanation":"Solution cavity liners and varnishes protect the interface between dentin and restorative material. Copal varnish and dentin desensitizers are listed; blocking dentinal tubules helps reduce sensitivity and the lecture associates them with amalgam. A cement base/liner is applied in thicker layers beneath restorations to provide mechanical support and thermal protection for pulp. In the cross-section, the thin liner lies close to dentin/pulp while the thicker base supports the overlying restoration. Identify enamel, dentin and pulp separately; a luting film beneath a crown is a different role.","q":"What is the difference between a protective liner and a base?","a":"A liner protects the dentin/pulp interface; a thicker base provides support and insulation.","original":"Solution Cavity Liners and varnishes:\n function as a protective barrier between dentin and the restorative material\n •  Copal Varnish\n •  Dentin desensitizers\n ( Block dentinal tubules and hence reduce sensitivity - Used under amalgam restoration)\n\n Cement base/liner : are applied in thicker layers beneath restorative materials to provide mechanical support for a\n restoration and thermal protection for the pulp."},{"p":9,"title":"Material-to-use table and Dycal","explanation":"The table maps Dycal to liner; glass ionomer and RMGIC to base, liner and temporary restoration; IRM to base, temporary restoration and (conflicting with p.6) permanent luting cement. Biodentine is listed for vital pulp therapy (pulp capping and pulpotomy) and repair of root/furcation perforations and internal/external resorption. Dycal contains calcium hydroxide, is used in deep cavities close to pulp, comes as two tubes (base/catalyst), and stimulates reparative dentin formation. The package in the original photo is a useful practical ID clue. Treat the conflicting IRM entry as a source issue and confirm with the instructor.","q":"What is Dycal's main component and purpose?","a":"Calcium hydroxide; liner near pulp that stimulates reparative dentin.","original":"Dycal                    Liner\n\nGlass ionomer         Base - Liner - Temporary restoration\n\nResin modified glass   Base - Liner - Temporary restoration\n\nionomer\n                    Base - Temporary restoration - Permanent luting cementIRM\n\nBiodentine               Vital pulp therapy; including pulp capping and pulpotomy\n                             In the root, it can be used for repairing root, furcation perforations, internal and\n                       external resorption\n\n\n1- Dycal:\n\n1.  Calcium hydroxide is the main component\n2. Used in deep cavities close to the pulp\n3. Comes as 2 tubes base and catalyst\n4.  Stimulates formation of reparative dentin"},{"p":10,"title":"Conventional GIC: ingredients and ratios","explanation":"Glass ionomer cement contains silicate glass powder and polyacrylic acid liquid. It is used as base, liner and temporary restoration. It releases fluoride and chemically retains to tooth; the lecture describes good seal and no microleakage, which is not a guarantee for every restoration. Memorize the lab ratios with their units: liner 1 scoop powder : 2 drops liquid; base 1 scoop powder : 1 scoop liquid; restorative 2 scoops powder : 1 scoop liquid. Capsule workflow: activate in activator, mix in amalgamator, dispense with applicator. Real formulation ratios and measuring devices depend on manufacturer.","q":"Give the three GIC ratios in the lab notes.","a":"Liner 1 scoop:2 drops; base 1 scoop:1 scoop; restorative 2 scoops:1 scoop.","original":"2- Glass ionomer cement:\n Main components: Powder: silicate glass\n                       liquid: Polyacrylic Acid\n\n Used as:\n\no A Base\no A liner\no A temporary restoration\n\nAdvantages :\n1. Release Fluoride\n2.Chemical Retention {so no micro leakage and ensures good seal}\n\nCOMES AS :\nPowder and liquid\nMixed in a ratio of 1 scoop powder / 2 drop liquid (liner)\nMixed in a ratio of 1scoop powder / 1scoop liquid (base)\nMixed in a ratio of 2 scoop powder / 1scoop liquid (restorative)\n\n Premixed capsule\n Activated in an activator\n Mixed in an amalgamator\n Dispensed using in applicator"},{"p":11,"title":"Resin-modified glass ionomer (RMGIC)","explanation":"The slide describes dual cure and high fluoride release. Uses: liner, base, temporary restoration, and permanent restoration in pediatric/geriatric patients. Powder-liquid and capsule forms are shown. The three lecture ratios are identical to page 10: liner one scoop powder/two drops liquid; base one scoop powder/one scoop liquid; restorative two scoops powder/one scoop liquid. Capsule sequence is activator, amalgamator, applicator. Recall that RMGIC includes resin modification, so curing behavior distinguishes it from conventional self-cure GIC.","q":"Which uses and curing features distinguish RMGIC in the lab slide?","a":"Dual cure, high fluoride release, liner/base/temporary and pediatric or geriatric permanent restoration.","original":"3- Resin Modified GIC (RMGIC) :\n•  Dual cure\n•  High fluoride release\n•  used as liner\n•  used as a base\n•  used as a temporary restoration\n•  used a permanent restoration in pediatric/geriatric patients\n\n\n   Comes as:-\n    Powderand liquid:\n        Mixed in a ratio of 1 scoop powder: 2drop liquid (liner)\n        Mixed in a ratio of 1scoop powder: 1 scoop liquid (base)\n        Mixed in a ration of 2 scoop powder: 1 scoop liquid (restorative)\n\n    Premixed in a capsule:\n          Activated in an activator\n        Mixed in an amalgamator\n         Dispensed using in applicator"},{"p":12,"title":"IRM and Biodentine: two very different roles","explanation":"Polymer-reinforced ZOE (IRM) has a sedative property and is used as a base under amalgam and temporary restoration; the slide again says permanent luting, in conflict with page 6. Biodentine comes as powder and liquid, promotes pulp healing, and is used as a base or liner. Its other vital pulp and repair uses appear on page 9. The photos show the different branded packages. Remember IRM is intermediate restorative material; Biodentine is a pulp-repair material. Neither label alone authorizes a clinical procedure.","q":"Compare IRM and Biodentine by primary material role.","a":"IRM: intermediate restoration/base; Biodentine: pulp healing, base/liner and repair uses.","original":"4- Polymer-reinforced zinc oxide-eugenol restorative cement (IRM)\n   Has a sedative property•\n   Used as a base under Amalgam•\n   Used as a permanent luting cement•\n   Used as a temporary Restoration•\n\n\n\n\n5- Biodentine\n•  Comes as powder and liquid\n•    It promotes pulp healing Used\n•  as a base or liner"},{"p":13,"title":"Equipment and product identification station","explanation":"Recognize all seven objects shown: self-cure glass ionomer bottles; adhesive resin cement clicker; Ketac conditioner; light-cure glaze varnish; applicator; amalgamator; activator. The activator prepares a capsule for mixing, the amalgamator mechanically mixes it, and the applicator dispenses it. The conditioner prepares the surface according to its product instructions and the glaze varnish is a protective coating. In an exam, say the name and what the device/material is for rather than naming only the brand.","q":"Put the three capsule instruments in order.","a":"Activator -> amalgamator -> applicator.","original":"Glass                          Adhesive resin cement             Ketacconditioner   Glazelight cure\n  (self                                                                                       varnishionomers\n  cure)\n\n\n\n\n\n     Applicator                     Amalgamator                        Activator"}]},"lab-waxes":{"title":"Dental Waxes","file":"6_Waxes lab _AC.pdf","pages":14,"groups":[[1,3,"Wax properties & classes"],[4,4,"Clinical waxes"],[5,8,"Inlay & casting patterns"],[9,9,"Baseplate wax"],[10,11,"Beading & boxing"],[12,14,"Other processing & impression waxes"]],"guide":[{"p":1,"title":"Dental waxes: laboratory orientation","explanation":"This lab covers identifying waxes by form, color and use, then sorting them into clinical, pattern, processing and impression functions. The most reliable exam clue is what the wax does, not just its color: many products come in more than one color.","q":"What should you identify besides the name of a wax?","a":"Its classification, characteristic form and use.","original":"DENTAL WAXES\n\n\nINSTRUCTOR: LOVELY\n\nGP INCHARGE: DR. ADLA AHMED"},{"p":2,"title":"Thermoplastic behavior and classification","explanation":"Waxes are thermoplastic materials normally solid at room temperature. Moderate heating melts them into mobile liquids without decomposition. On cooling they become solid again. The slide classifies natural waxes by mineral, plant, insect and animal origin; synthetic waxes are listed as petroleum products in this handout. By chemical composition, the categories are hydrocarbons and esters. The photographs show examples of natural sources and wax granules. Learn these two classification systems separately from dental use.","q":"What two chemical groups are used to classify waxes?","a":"Hydrocarbons and esters.","original":"Waxes are thermoplastic materials which are normally solid at room temperature, but when exposed to\nmoderate temperatures, melt without decomposition to form mobile liquids.\n\n\n\nClassification based on :\n\nI . Origin\n   1- Natural : Mineral\n               Plant\n                 Insect\n             Animal\n   2- Synthetic : Petroleum products\n\n\n\n\n\nII . Chemical composition\n   1- Hydrocarbons\n   2- Esters"},{"p":3,"title":"Classification by dental use","explanation":"Clinical waxes: bite registration, disclosing and utility. Laboratory pattern waxes: inlay, casting and baseplate. Processing waxes: utility/beading, boxing, sticky and block-out. Impression waxes: corrective and bite registration. Some names appear in more than one group (utility, bite registration), because the classification describes the role in a particular workflow. Pattern wax models the shape to be made; processing wax assists fabrication; impression wax records or corrects a surface.","q":"Name the four processing waxes.","a":"Utility/beading, boxing, sticky and block-out.","original":"III . Dental uses\n\n   1- clinical use : Bite registration wax\n                   Disclosing wax\n                      Utility wax\n\n  2- lab. use : 1*Pattern wax\n                  Inlay wax\n                Casting wax\n                Baseplate\n            wax\n              2*Processing wax\n                     Utility wax beading\n                Boxing wax\n                  Sticky wax\n                 Block out wax\n\n              3*Impression wax\n                 Corrective wax\n                   Bite registration"},{"p":4,"title":"Clinical waxes: bite, disclose and adapt","explanation":"Bite registration wax contains beeswax, paraffin, ceresin and aluminum or copper particles for stability. It records occlusal tooth surfaces and the relationship between upper and lower teeth. Disclosing wax is painted on the tissue side of a denture base; pressure makes wax flow away from pressure points that need relief. The slide also calls it pressure indicator paste. Utility wax (periphery wax) gives a desirable contour to a tray. Look at the registration sheets, disclosing wax container and red tray-periphery wax in the original images.","q":"Which wax locates pressure points and which changes tray contour?","a":"Disclosing wax locates pressure points; utility/periphery wax contours the tray.","original":"Bite registration dental wax                    Disclosing dental wax:\n\n\nComposition : Bees wax, paraffin wax,\nceresin, aluminum or copper particles (give stability).\n\nUses : record occlusal surface of teeth and the relation\nbetween upper and lower teeth."},{"p":5,"title":"Inlay pattern wax: direct and indirect","explanation":"Type 1 is softened and used directly in the mouth. Type 2 is more frequently used and is melted onto a die outside the mouth (indirect technique). A die is a positive replica of a prepared tooth; a wax pattern models the desired shape before fabrication. The blue stick and purple wax in the photographs illustrate different presentations. Distinguish the wax pattern from the finished restoration.","q":"Which inlay wax type is direct and which is indirect?","a":"Type 1 direct in mouth; Type 2 indirect on a die outside mouth.","original":"Inlay Pattern Dental Wax :\nType 1 : softened and used directly in the mouth.\n\n\n\n\n\nType 2 : more frequently used, the wax is melted onto a die outside the mouth in the indirect technique."},{"p":6,"title":"Casting pattern wax: framework shapes","explanation":"Casting pattern wax constructs the metal framework of partial dentures. The slide shows multiple colors and prefabricated shapes including clasps, mesh and bars. These pieces model the framework before investing and casting. A clasp can engage a tooth, mesh provides a patterned retention area, and bars connect framework sections. These are conceptual shape explanations; the photo shows the actual materials for identification.","q":"What is casting pattern wax used to construct?","a":"Metal frameworks for partial dentures; available as clasps, mesh and bars.","original":"Casting Pattern Dental Wax\nUsed to: construct the metal framework of partial dentures\nAvailable in different colors and shapes { clasps ,mesh ,bars }"},{"p":7,"title":"Recognize prefabricated casting wax patterns","explanation":"The visual-only slide shows a black sheet of clasp-like forms (Plastiform), green prefabricated clasp patterns (rewax), and red/green mesh sheets. These are wax patterns used for partial denture framework construction, not finished metal components. Different colors are manufacturer-dependent. Recognize the physical form and name its role in the pattern stage.","q":"What are the perforated red and green wax sheets used for?","a":"Casting pattern mesh for partial denture frameworks.","original":""},{"p":8,"title":"Sprue wax and the lost-wax idea","explanation":"The visual slide shows coiled sprue wax, a crown pattern attached to a sprue inside an investment ring, and a finished metal crown. The ring diagram labels magnesium phosphate cement/investment, crown, sprue and ring mold. The sprue is the channel that connects the wax pattern to the casting entry; after wax elimination it provides a route for casting material. This is a simplified interpretation of the depicted workflow, not casting parameters or a laboratory protocol.","q":"What is the function of a sprue in the casting diagram?","a":"Creates a channel connecting the crown pattern space to the casting entry.","original":""},{"p":9,"title":"Baseplate pattern wax: occlusal rims","explanation":"Baseplate wax is used to make occlusal rims for complete dentures. The four features established are vertical dimension, occlusal plane, initial arch form and denture contour. Vertical dimension concerns separation of the jaws; occlusal plane concerns the orientation of the biting surfaces; arch form and contour describe the future denture shape. The photos show pink/red sheets, wax rims on casts, and rims with arranged denture teeth.","q":"What four features do baseplate wax occlusal rims establish?","a":"Vertical dimension, occlusal plane, initial arch form, denture contour.","original":"Baseplate Pattern Dental Wax\nUsed in :\nOcclusal rims in complete denture to establish : vertical dimension\n                                                  occlusal plane\n                                                              initial arch form\n                                           denture contour"},{"p":10,"title":"Utility processing wax: beading","explanation":"Utility wax customizes tray borders. During beading it preserves the border of a final impression. The lab specifies keeping it 2 mm below the border so the vestibule will be clear after pouring. The vestibule is the space between cheek/lip and the ridge or teeth. Observe the red bead around the edge of a tray or impression. Do not confuse beading (a narrow edge strip) with boxing (a surrounding wall).","q":"How far below the impression border is the bead placed in the lab slide?","a":"2 mm, to preserve a clear vestibule in the cast.","original":"Utility Processing Wax (Beading)\n\n\nUsed to Customize borders of the tray.\nBeading : Preserve the border of final impression (Should be kept below the border by 2 mm so\nthe vestibule will be clear after pouring"},{"p":11,"title":"Boxing processing wax","explanation":"Boxing wax surrounds the utility bead to form the base portion of the poured cast. It creates a wall that contains the gypsum and helps form a smooth cast with adequate thickness. The photos show broad red wax sheets and cylindrical walls around upper and lower impressions. The bead maintains the border region; the box controls the cast base. Think edge versus wall.","q":"What is the purpose of boxing wax?","a":"Surrounds the bead to contain the cast mix and form a smooth, thicker cast base.","original":"Boxing Processing Wax\n\nIt will form the base portion of the poured cast\n(It will cover the area all around the utility wax).\nMake the cast smooth & Increases the thickness of the cast."},{"p":12,"title":"Sticky processing wax","explanation":"Sticky wax is made from resin and wax, and the lab identifies it as yellow. It temporarily fixes components during fabrication or repair. The images show a yellow stick and a split denture temporarily stabilized. It is not a permanent repair material. Its name describes its temporary fixation purpose.","q":"What are the composition, color and use of sticky wax?","a":"Resin and wax; yellow; temporary fixation during fabrication and repair.","original":"Sticky Processing Wax\nComposition: resin and wax\nYellow in color\nUsed for temporary fixation during fabrication & repair."},{"p":13,"title":"Block-out processing wax","explanation":"Block-out wax fills undercuts, creates clasp steps and relieves critical areas of a model. Filling a recessed area changes the shape so a subsequent component does not lock into it. The images show wax forms, a model with selected areas covered and a block-out application. It is a processing wax, not an impression or casting pattern wax.","q":"List the three uses of block-out wax.","a":"Block undercuts, create clasp steps, relieve critical model areas.","original":"Block out Processing Wax\nThis wax was developed for blocking out undercuts, creating\nclasp steps and relieving critical areas of the model."},{"p":14,"title":"Corrective and bite registration impression waxes","explanation":"Corrective impression wax is used with another impression material in edentulous impressions to record the posterior palatal seal. It adds or corrects details rather than making an entire new impression. Bite registration impression wax records the relationship of the arches; the slide shows several wax wafer forms. The posterior palatal seal is the posterior border region of an upper complete denture. The two waxes share the impression category but record different things.","q":"Which impression wax records the posterior palatal seal?","a":"Corrective impression wax.","original":"Corrective Impression Wax\nUsed with other impression materials in process of\ntaking edentulous impression record posterior palatal seal\n\n\n\n\n\nBite Registration Impression Wax"}]},"lab-preventive":{"title":"Preventive Dentistry","file":"7_Preventive dentistry lab _AC.pdf","pages":10,"groups":[[1,3,"Prevention & fluoride classes"],[4,4,"Systemic fluoride"],[5,5,"Topical fluoride"],[6,7,"Gel, prophy paste & varnish"],[8,9,"Fissure sealant"],[10,10,"Regular dental visits"]],"guide":[{"p":1,"title":"Preventive dentistry: laboratory orientation","explanation":"The lab focuses on fluoride and fissure sealants, their forms, indications and the illustrated steps. The original images show recognizable product packaging, trays, varnish applicators and sealant syringes. Study the instructor's sequence and numeric facts separately from actual patient treatment decisions.","q":"What are the two main prevention tools covered in this lecture?","a":"Fluoride and fissure sealant.","original":"PREVENTIVE DENTISTRY\n\n\nINSTRUCTOR: DR. LOVELY\n\nGP INCHARGE: DR. ADLA AHMED"},{"p":2,"title":"Preventing dental caries","explanation":"Preventive dentistry aims to prevent dental caries and is a joint effort among the dentist, hygienist and patient. This lecture focuses on fluoride and fissure sealant. Fluoride products are presented as water fluoridation, toothpaste, supplements, mouth rinse, gel, prophylaxis paste and varnish. Sealants physically cover vulnerable pits and fissures. The photo montage is an introduction to the different forms.","q":"Who participates in preventive dentistry?","a":"Dentist, hygienist and patient.","original":"What is Preventive Dentistry?\nDentistry thatis aimedat preventing dental caries.\nIt is a joint effort between the dentist, the hygienist, and the patient.\n\nSome ways which dental caries can be prevented by:\n1.  Fluoride\n2.  Fissure sealant"},{"p":3,"title":"Systemic vs topical fluoride","explanation":"The source table separates systemic fluoride into water fluoridation and dietary fluoride supplements; topical fluoride into self-applied and professionally applied. Systemic refers to ingestion or systemic exposure, while topical refers to contact with tooth surfaces. Some systemic delivery also has topical effects during use. For the lab classification, preserve the exact four categories and their branches.","q":"What are the two systemic and two topical categories?","a":"Systemic: water fluoridation and supplements. Topical: self-applied and professionally applied.","original":""},{"p":4,"title":"Systemic fluoride: water and the supplement table","explanation":"Water fluoridation adjusts the fluoride content of deficient drinking water to a recommended level; the slide states 0.7 mg/L. Dietary supplements are prescribed for high-caries-risk children aged 6 months to 16 years and are shown as drops, tablets, lozenges and chewing gum. The slide's historical dosage table has three water levels: less than 0.3 ppm, 0.3-0.6 ppm and greater than 0.6 ppm. Birth-6 months: none at all levels. 6 months-3 years: 0.25 mg/day only in the lowest category. 3-6 years: 0.50 mg/day, 0.25 mg/day, none. 6-16 years: 1.0 mg/day, 0.50 mg/day, none. Study this as a reproduced lecture table, not a personalized dosing calculator; actual prescribing requires assessment of total fluoride exposure, risk and current guidance by a clinician.","q":"What drinking-water level is stated, and which age group receives no supplements in all columns?","a":"0.7 mg/L; birth to 6 months.","original":"Systemic Fluoride\nWater Fluoridation:\nThe process of  adjusting the  fluoride content of  fluoride-deficient\nwater  to  the  recommended  level  for  optimal  dental  health.\nRecomended level of fluoride in drinking water is 0.7 milligrams per\nLiter (mg/L).\n\n\n\n\n\n❑Dietary fluoride supplements:\nPrescribed for high caries risk children ages ( 6 months to\n16 years ) of age.\n *Tablets, lozenges, drops, chewing gums"},{"p":5,"title":"Topical fluoride: self and professional forms","explanation":"Self-applied topical forms include fluoride toothpaste, mouth rinses and gels. Professionally applied forms include rinses, gels or foams at higher concentrations than self-applied products, fluoride-containing prophylaxis paste and varnish. The photographs illustrate toothpaste, rinse, tray gel, disposable prophy paste cups and a professional applicator. The difference is delivery and concentration, not simply whether the product looks like a liquid.","q":"List the professional topical fluoride forms in this lecture.","a":"Rinse, gel, foam, fluoride prophy paste and fluoride varnish.","original":"Topical fluoride :\n\n\n ❑Self applied topical fluoride:\n     Fluoride containing toothpaste.\n     Fluoride Mouth rinse or Gels.\n\n\n\n ❑Professionally Applied topical fluorides:\n    Fluoride Mouth rinse, Gels, or Foams (Higher concentration than the self-applied fluorides)\n    Fluoride-Containing Prophylaxis Paste.\n    Fluoride Varnish."},{"p":6,"title":"Fluoride gel: the lecture sequence","explanation":"Gel is carried using a disposable tray and comes in different colors and flavors. The lab sequence is: squeeze gel into the tray and leave in the mouth for 1 minute; ask the patient to spit out the material without rinsing; no eating for 30 minutes. The photograph shows double-arch disposable trays and gel dispensing. These exact times are the instructor's slide values; they vary by product and clinical situation, so do not apply them as universal patient instructions.","q":"What three timed/behavior steps does the gel slide state?","a":"Tray gel 1 min, spit without rinsing, no eating for 30 min (lecture protocol).","original":"Fluoride Gel Professionally Applied Topical Fluorides\n\n•  Carried to the oral cavity using a disposable\n   tray Available in different colors and flavors\n\nSteps for fluoride gel application:\n1.Squeeze the gel into the tray and leave it in patients mouth for 1 minute\n2.Ask the patient to spit out material (do not rinse)\n 3.Patient should not eat for 30 minutes"},{"p":7,"title":"Prophylaxis paste and varnish","explanation":"Fluoride-containing prophylaxis paste is used after calculus removal (scaling) and the lecture says it reduces sensitivity. Fluoride varnish is described as closing dentinal tubules and reducing sensitivity. The slide states first layer blocks 55% of tubules and second closes 85%. These are lecture-specific percentages, not universal clinical performance measurements. The original photographs show colored prophy paste cups and varnish brushed onto teeth.","q":"What tubule-blocking percentages are stated for one and two varnish layers?","a":"55% and 85%, respectively, according to the slide.","original":"Fluoride-Containing Prophylaxis Paste Professionally Applied Topical\nFluorides\n\n•    It's used after removal of calculus (scaling )\n•    It reduces sensitivity\n\n\n\n\n\nFlouride Varnish Professionally Applied Topical Fluorides\n\n    •  Used to close dentinal tubules\n         1st layer blocks 55% of dentinal tubules\n       2nd layer closes 85%\n    •  Reduces sensitivity since It contains fluoride"},{"p":8,"title":"Fissure sealant: purpose and steps","explanation":"The lab says sealants are used mainly in deep non-carious pits and fissures of children's permanent teeth. They cover vulnerable grooves. The lecture sequence: clean the tooth; etch for 15 sec, then wash and dry; apply pit and fissure sealant; light cure 20-30 sec. The six-photo diagram separates the clean/dry and etch stages and shows the final sealant. Memorize the slide sequence and times, but actual etching, curing and isolation requirements depend on the product and supervised clinical assessment.","q":"Give the four sealant steps and the stated times.","a":"Clean; etch 15 s, wash/dry; apply; light cure 20-30 s.","original":"Fissure sealant\n\n  - Used mainly in deep pits and fissures that are not carious in children's permanent teeth.\n\n\n\n\nSteps of application:\n1.  Clean the tooth\n2.  Etch tooth for 15 sec then wash and dry\n3. Apply pit and fissure sealant\n4.  Light cure for 20 to 30 sec"},{"p":9,"title":"Recognize the before-and-after appearance","explanation":"The four visual comparisons show deep occlusal grooves before sealant and a smoother filled groove after sealant. Look for the material covering pits and fissures rather than an entire crown. The diagram emphasizes the physical barrier. The image is a recognition exercise; it does not establish whether a particular tooth is clinically eligible for sealant.","q":"What visual change should you recognize after a sealant is placed?","a":"Pits and fissures are filled/covered, producing a smoother protected groove.","original":""},{"p":10,"title":"Why regular dental visits matter","explanation":"The closing infographic highlights three benefits for children: healthy development of teeth and gums, early detection of oral health problems and promotion of good oral hygiene habits. It lists evaluation of loose teeth, cavities, gum health, bite habits, tongue and speech, fluoride use, diet and health history, and home care and prevention. Children can learn brushing, flossing and tooth counting. Prevention is broader than fluoride or sealants; it also involves regular monitoring and daily habits.","q":"What three benefits of regular dental visits are highlighted?","a":"Healthy development, early problem detection, and good oral hygiene habits.","original":""}]}};
